{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":22417,"items":[{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7607644","title":["XBRIDGE: Cross-Domain Bridge Distillation for Data-Efficient Video Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Dimitar","family":"Atanasov","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5993-1665","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Taolue","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2494-5127","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Stelios","family":"Sotiriadis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Compact video classifiers often underfit on small, domain-specific datasets, while mismatched pretraining sources can limit direct knowledge distillation. We propose Cross-Domain Bridge Distillation (XBRIDGE), which transfers knowledge from a teacher through an assistant pretrained on a semantically adjacent video domain to a compact student. On XD-Violence, a Kinetics-400 assistant achieves higher standalone accuracy (86.26%) but produces a weaker student (68.39%) than a cross-domain Something-Something V2 assistant (72.95%). This suggests that assistant accuracy is a poor proxy for transfer quality and motivates investigating temporal and object-interaction priors for low-data targets. A lightweight Contextual Bridge Optimiser supports assistant and supervision-weight selection under a fixed compute budget. Evaluation on binary violence detection and multiclass sports recognition shows gains over direct fine-tuning and direct distillation. We report parameters, FLOPs, and latency alongside classification performance to characterise the trade-offs for compact video transformers.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7607644","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T16:28:36Z","timestamp":1791908916000}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7508159","title":["When Does Model Compression Pay Off on a CPU? A Controlled Study of Quantization, Pruning and Distillation for Text Classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-7581-6306","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Taofiq","family":"Ilahi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Compression choices for CPU inference are often made from one benchmark run that reports one accuracy and one latency number per technique. We test how far such numbers can be trusted by compressing a fine-tuned DistilBERT classifier on AG News with INT8 dynamic quantization, one-shot magnitude pruning and knowledge distillation, and evaluating every variant on the full 7,600-example test set with bootstrap intervals, paired tests, interleaved repeated timing (batch sizes 1 to 64, one and six threads), a kernel-level analysis, and a distillation grid over student depth, transfer data, loss and random seeds. INT8 quantization shrinks the model by 48% for 0.44 points of accuracy, but its speed benefit depends on the setting: it is 1.9\u00d7 faster on one thread and slower than FP32 (0.93\u00d7) at batch size 1 on six threads, because the INT8 linear kernels barely speed up with more threads at small inputs (1.0 to 1.3\u00d7 from one to six threads) while FP32 kernels speed up 2.4 to 2.9\u00d7. Pruning without retraining is harmless only up to about 19% whole-model sparsity and gives no size or speed benefit in dense tensors. Distillation is what delivers latency: a 2-layer student trained with the teacher's soft labels on 16,000 examples reaches 89.1% accuracy at 3.2 ms against 74.6 ms for the baseline on one thread, and the soft labels are worth 3.6 to 4.8 points over training on labels alone. A first single-run benchmark suggested that a deeper student scores worse; per-seed results show this was an optimization failure, since under fixed hyperparameters five of sixteen 4-layer runs failed (two never started to learn) against none of sixteen 2-layer runs, and with training stabilized the deeper student is still not better. Code and every result file are public.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7508159","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T08:25:44Z","timestamp":1790238344000}},{"abstract":"<jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>\n                      Shrimp disease classification has become an urgent issue due to its significant impact on the import\u2013export output of producing countries, particularly Vietnam. Most existing studies focus on image-based classification, which typically operates at the late stage of disease manifestation. Therefore, text-based classification has the potential to enable early and timely disease detection. To address this limitation, we introduce the SALT (\n                      <jats:bold>S<\/jats:bold>\n                      hrimp disease text\n                      <jats:bold>A<\/jats:bold>\n                      nalysis with multi-\n                      <jats:bold>L<\/jats:bold>\n                      oss dis\n                      <jats:bold>T<\/jats:bold>\n                      illation) framework, which incorporates explainability analysis using Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) and SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) to evaluate model predictions and interpret the learned linguistic features. Experimental results demonstrate that SALT achieves competitive performance across multiple distillation objectives, outperforming supervised baselines while providing a favorable trade-off between predictive performance and computational efficiency. Moreover, it exhibits strong explainability, accurately identifying key linguistic features and semantic patterns relevant to disease descriptions. These findings highlight the potential of knowledge distillation\u2013based text classification for future applications in early shrimp disease diagnosis and related research directions.\n                    <\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>","DOI":"10.1007\/s44443-026-01271-8","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T13:12:00Z","timestamp":1791465120000},"title":["Explainable multi-loss distillation framework for efficient and interpretable shrimp disease text classification"],"author":[{"given":"Quynh Anh","family":"Nguyen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Quoc Khang","family":"Nguyen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5661-4250","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Luyl-Da","family":"Quach","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s44443-026-01271-8.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/article\/10.1007\/s44443-026-01271-8","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s44443-026-01271-8.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s44443-026-01271-8","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Compact deep models are attractive for medical image classification, but conventional knowledge distillation mainly transfers class-level predictions and may not adequately preserve lesion-relevant spatial cues. To address this limitation, we propose LaHKD, a lesion-aware hierarchical knowledge distillation framework for parameter-efficient medical image classification. LaHKD enables a lightweight student to learn hierarchical semantic representations together with lesion-focused guidance from a stronger teacher. We evaluate LaHKD on HAM10000 dermoscopic lesion classification and a brain tumor MRI classification benchmark. Across both datasets, LaHKD improves compact-student classification performance, with the clearest lesion-focused spatial benefits observed on HAM10000, where lesion morphology is central to diagnosis and direct lesion supervision is available. On the magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) benchmark, localization analysis is limited to an auxiliary recovered-mask subset and is therefore interpreted as exploratory; under this setting, consistent localization gains are not observed. Overall, LaHKD provides an effective framework for compact medical image classification, with spatial benefits most clearly supported in tasks with reliable lesion supervision.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/jimaging12090437","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T14:39:24Z","timestamp":1789137564000},"title":["Improving Parameter-Efficient Medical Image Classification with Lesion-Aware Hierarchical Knowledge Distillation"],"author":[{"given":"Yarong","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Guilin University of Technology, Guilin 541006, China"},{"name":"Guangxi Key Laboratory of Embedded Technology and Intelligent System, Guilin University of Technology, Guilin 541006, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Runmei","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Guilin University of Technology, Guilin 541006, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaolan","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Guilin University of Technology, Guilin 541006, China"},{"name":"Guangxi Key Laboratory of Embedded Technology and Intelligent System, Guilin University of Technology, Guilin 541006, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Huilin","family":"Zheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Guilin University of Technology, Guilin 541006, China"},{"name":"Guangxi Key Laboratory of Embedded Technology and Intelligent System, Guilin University of Technology, Guilin 541006, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2313-433X\/12\/9\/437\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/jimaging12090437","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.nexres.2026.102475","title":["Reliability-aware transformer-to-CNN knowledge distillation for efficient cotton leaf disease classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5731-6042","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Faisal","family":"Islam","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-4447-0947","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Partha Jyoti","family":"Roy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-0086-5019","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hrithik Mohanto","family":"Himel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.nexres.2026.102475","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,11]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T15:12:31Z","timestamp":1790176351000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S3050475926011644?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S3050475926011644?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3829641.3829698","title":["A Short Text Classification Method Based on Multi-level Feature Alignment and Decoupled Knowledge Distillation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-2894-374X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Qiang","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Information Engineering, Dalian University, Dalian, Liaoning, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-4158-9526","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Taiji","family":"Pan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Information Engineering, Dalian University, Dalian, Liaoning, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5019-9584","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hanhan","family":"Dang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Information Engineering, Dalian University, Dalian, Liaoning, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3829641.3829698","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T06:42:51Z","timestamp":1789540971000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3829641.3829698","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.patrec.2026.09.019","title":["DBD-KD: Dual-branch disentangled knowledge distillation for efficient crack classification"],"author":[{"given":"Jinlong","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mimi","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuyan","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7465-9882","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaoyun","family":"Ye","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2842-3526","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Haoran","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.patrec.2026.09.019","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,11]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T15:34:39Z","timestamp":1789572879000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0167865526003363?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0167865526003363?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.displa.2026.103679","title":["Parameter-efficient image\u2013text classification via bidirectional fusion and directional disagreement refinement"],"author":[{"given":"Phuong","family":"Lam","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Y","family":"Nguyen Minh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6175-3522","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Thien Khai","family":"Tran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.displa.2026.103679","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]],"date-time":"2026-09-08T06:36:21Z","timestamp":1788849381000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0141938226003422?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0141938226003422?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tensymp68909.2026.11704062","title":["Multi-Expert EfficientNet Forests with Lightweight Knowledge Distillation for Resource-Efficient Vehicle Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Md. Mahmudul","family":"Hasan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent University","department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Abdullah","family":"Rahman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University","department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Umme Mahbuba","family":"Jannat","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University","department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Khorshed","family":"Alam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universite Laval","department":["Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tarem","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent University","department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mahbubul Haq","family":"Bhuiyan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University","department":["Department of Computer Science and Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tensymp68909.2026.11704062","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:17:03Z","timestamp":1790623023000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11703968\/11703969\/11704062.pdf?arnumber=11704062","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/acit69831.2026.11700134","title":["A Cost-Efficient Routing Pipeline for Multilingual Short-Text Classification Using Small Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0939-016X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wajdi","family":"Ben Saad","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Carthago Labs","place":["Paris, France"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-1025-0887","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Safa","family":"Madiouni","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universit\u00e9 Paris Dauphine-PSL","place":["Paris, France"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/acit69831.2026.11700134","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:19:16Z","timestamp":1790623156000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11699778\/11699878\/11700134.pdf?arnumber=11700134","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1021\/acs.jctc.6c00917.s001","title":["Data-Efficient and Fast Machine Learning Molecular Dynamics through Integrated Active Learning and Knowledge Distillation"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1021\/acs.jctc.6c00917.s001","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]],"date-time":"2026-09-08T12:19:08Z","timestamp":1788869948000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.1021\/acs.jctc.6c00917","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mapr72750.2026.11685690","title":["Representation-Aware Knowledge Distillation for Efficient Malware Family Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Minh-Quan","family":"Duong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Science","place":["Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Thanh-Nhan","family":"Tran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Science","place":["Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kieu-Oanh","family":"Nguyen-Ngoc","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Science","place":["Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Minh-Quan","family":"Ho-Le","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Science","place":["Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nhut-Thanh","family":"Le-Hinh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Science","place":["Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Minh-Triet","family":"Tran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Science","place":["Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mapr72750.2026.11685690","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T19:21:24Z","timestamp":1789586484000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11685436\/11685665\/11685690.pdf?arnumber=11685690","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4127\/table-7","title":["Table 7: Performance of various models for text classification."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4127\/table-7","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,14]],"date-time":"2026-10-14T08:50:23Z","timestamp":1791967823000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4127","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Network attack decoy systems are widely deployed to lure adversaries and collect detailed attack behaviors. However, the massive and heterogeneous logs they generate, together with the imbalance between normal and attack traces, make it difficult to build reliable automatic classifiers. Motivated by these challenges, in this paper we propose an efficient attack classification decoy system to improve the automatic network attack classification capability of decoy-based defences. In this paper, we propose an efficient attack classification strategy in decoy systems, based on an ensemble learning approach with text-based data augmentation, to address these issues. To elucidate, an attack sequence generation method is proposed to simplify the logs and transform the attack classification problem into a text categorization problem. Additionally, we tackle the data imbalance issue by employing data augmentation on the extracted numerical features from the text. Moreover, we construct our classification model based on ensemble learning, utilizing various classifiers in conjunction with diverse features. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach improves accuracy and precision by over 10% in attack classification, significantly improving classification effectiveness. Furthermore, we deployed our system in a real-world environment to validate its performance. The results reveal notable accomplishments in attack deception and attack classification.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1093\/comjnl\/bxag098","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T11:08:05Z","timestamp":1788520085000},"title":["Efficient attack classification in decoy systems: an ensemble learning approach with text-based data augmentation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6331-9383","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Peng","family":"Gao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Cyber Science and Engineering, Nanjing University of Science and Technology , No. 200 Xiaolingwei Street, Nanjing 210094, Jiangsu ,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuangquan","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Cyber Science and Engineering, Nanjing University of Science and Technology , No. 200 Xiaolingwei Street, Nanjing 210094, Jiangsu ,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ziyu","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Cyber Science and Engineering, Sichuan University , No. 2, Section 2, Chuanda Road, Chengdu 610207, Sichuan ,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sirui","family":"Yao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Cyber Science and Engineering, Nanjing University of Science and Technology , No. 200 Xiaolingwei Street, Nanjing 210094, Jiangsu ,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jie","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Cyber Science and Engineering, Sichuan University , No. 2, Section 2, Chuanda Road, Chengdu 610207, Sichuan ,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Junjiang","family":"He","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Cyber Science and Engineering, Sichuan University , No. 2, Section 2, Chuanda Road, Chengdu 610207, Sichuan ,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/academic.oup.com\/comjnl\/advance-article-pdf\/doi\/10.1093\/comjnl\/bxag098\/71049324\/bxag098.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"syndication"},{"URL":"https:\/\/academic.oup.com\/comjnl\/advance-article-pdf\/doi\/10.1093\/comjnl\/bxag098\/71049324\/bxag098.pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/comjnl\/bxag098","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,13]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Traditional knowledge distillation transfers information from a large teacher model to a compact student by aligning static output distributions or internal representations for fixed inputs. Such formulations overlook how predictions change in response to input perturbations, even though robustness is critical for deployment. We interpret distillation as the transfer of functional response behavior under input variations and propose cross\u2010corruption distillation (CCD), which aligns corruption\u2010induced differences in teacher and student output via a Kullback\u2013Leibler objective. Rather than matching absolute logits, CCD distills how the teacher redistributes its prediction mass under perturbations, transferring teacher\u2010conditioned input sensitivity without architectural correspondence. On diverse multimodal benchmarks, CCD performs competitively, often outperforming conventional logit\u2010based distillation. It also slows performance degradation as perturbation strength increases and remains stable across corruption strategies and training schedules. These results position CCD as a response\u2010aware distillation framework integrating robustness directly into the distillation objective.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.4218\/etrij.2026-0159","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,21]],"date-time":"2026-09-21T07:11:48Z","timestamp":1789974708000},"title":["Cross\u2010corruption distillation for robust instruction following under text corruptions"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0227-8933","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yongjin","family":"Bae","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Visual Intelligence Research Section Electronics and Telecommunications Research Institute  Daejeon Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9007-4027","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kyoungman","family":"Bae","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Visual Intelligence Research Section Electronics and Telecommunications Research Institute  Daejeon Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/doi\/pdf\/10.4218\/etrij.2026-0159","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/doi\/full-xml\/10.4218\/etrij.2026-0159","content-type":"application\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/doi\/pdf\/10.4218\/etrij.2026-0159","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4218\/etrij.2026-0159","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,21]]}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Continual image-text retrieval (CITR) aims to accomplish the cross-modal retrieval job in the form of sequential multimedia data instead of single entire dataset. Due to the multi-granularity and semantic misalignment of vision and language modalities, CITR exhibits three challenges: spatial disorder of cross-modal data, heterogeneous structure of semantics and hierarchy of knowledge of cross-modal data, and knowledge aliasing during incremental sessions. The key insight is that traditional CITR methods use model the token-by-token similarity, which fails to capture fine-grained semantics especially when knowledge experiences multiple modalities and different incremental sessions. From the coalitional game perspective, we observe that each pairwise tokens of images and texts can act as coalition participants, the Banzhaf valuation can be modeled to characterize multi-grained semantics of image-text pairs. Based on this idea, we propose the Banzhaf Distillation Network (BDNet) for CITR. The core component of BDNet is the Banzhaf Distillation module, which preserves the structural cross-modal knowledge with the Banzhaf value, and retains the cross-task structural knowledge with a token-level similarity closeness constraint. Essentially, the Banzhaf valuation models the high order relation of image tokens and text tokens by using their interaction with respect to other tokens towards the semantic alignment of two modalities. Extensive experiments on several single-set scenarios and multi-set scenarios demonstrate the effectiveness of BDNet under single-set and multi-set scenarios. Code is available at https:\/\/github.com\/wangsfan\/CBD.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10760417\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T13:52:21Z","timestamp":1790689941000},"title":["Continual Image-Text Retrieval via Banzhaf Distillation from the Coalitional Game Perspective"],"author":[{"given":"Lei","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fuhao","family":"Wei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shaofan","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yanfeng","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-10760417\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-10760417\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10760417\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7422882","title":["Class-Structure Preservation Beats Diversity: A Comprehensive Benchmark of Text Augmentation Methods for Imbalanced Text Classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-6854-7231","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Keito","family":"Inoshita","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>With the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs), generative data augmentation has attracted considerable attention for imbalanced text classification in natural language processing. However, no empirical benchmark to date has compared LLM-based augmentation against the embedding-space SMOTE-style retrieval (EmbSMOTE), a strong classical reference for imbalanced classification. In this study, a controlled benchmark of 11 augmentation methods, spanning classical perturbation, embedding-space retrieval, and LLM-based generation, is newly constructed on seven public text classification datasets covering class counts K=2 to 28 and imbalance ratios of 1.1 to over 500, evaluated with five random seeds per cell using macro F1, Welch&amp;apos;s t-tests, five distributional metrics, and an LLM-family sensitivity analysis based on Qwen3-8B. The experimental results reveal that all LLM-based methods are statistically equivalent or inferior to EmbSMOTE, with the performance gap widening monotonically as imbalance increases and reaching \u0394F1macro \u2248 0.063 on GoEmotions-28. Furthermore, it is observed that surface-level uniqueness has negligible correlation with downstream performance, whereas LLM-specific artifacts, such as text elongation and label-distribution uniformization, are negatively associated with classification accuracy. Compared with six LLM-based and four classical augmentation baselines, these results demonstrate that the effective variable is not surface-level diversity but class-conditional structural fidelity, namely the degree to which augmented samples preserve the class-conditioned geometry of the training distribution. Accordingly, retrieval-based oversampling should be adopted as the default for imbalanced multi-class classification, and a higher empirical bar should be required before LLM-based augmentation is deployed in practice.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7422882","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,6]],"date-time":"2026-09-06T13:50:28Z","timestamp":1788702628000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tits.2026.3730377","title":["Multistage Simultaneous Knowledge Distillation Driven Lightweight and Efficient Broad Learning System for 3-D Object Classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8884-2170","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yifei","family":"Tian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University of Posts and Telecommunications","place":["Nanjing, Jiangsu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-7384-8380","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Min","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University of Posts and Telecommunications","place":["Nanjing, Jiangsu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7371-7465","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhengshan","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Karolinska Institutet","place":["Stockholm, Sweden"],"department":["Department of Clinical Science"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0184-5446","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Long","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Macau","place":["Macau, China"],"department":["Department of Computer and Information Science"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1864-3894","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jian","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University of Posts and Telecommunications","place":["Nanjing, Jiangsu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-2878-1853","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jieming","family":"Yin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University of Posts and Telecommunications","place":["Nanjing, Jiangsu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tits.2026.3730377","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T19:04:38Z","timestamp":1789585478000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/6979\/4358928\/11694263.pdf?arnumber=11694263","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7429770","title":["Efficient Diffusion Distillation via Embedding Loss"],"author":[{"given":"Jincheng","family":"Ying","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yitao","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenlin","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Minghui","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-8110-3939","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Yinhao","family":"Xiao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Diffusion distillation converts expensive iterative diffusion samplers into few-step or one-step generators. However, distribution-matching distillation often relies on large batches and long training schedules, which are difficult to sustain on limited hardware. We introduce Embedding Loss (EL), an online auxiliary Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) objective that compares real and generated samples in randomly initialized feature spaces. The feature extractors are resampled at every optimizer update and remain fixed during that update. EL requires neither pre-generated teacher outputs nor an adversarial discriminator. We analyze the stochastic gradient of distribution matching and separate batch-size-dependent Monte Carlo error from update-shared randomness, motivating EL as a complementary distribution-level signal. Experiments across CIFAR-10, ImageNet at 64*64 and 512*512 resolution, AFHQ-v2, and FFHQ show that EL is most beneficial when the training batch is small. It improves Fr&amp;apos;echet Inception Distance (FID) or convergence speed when combined with distribution-matching and Consistency Distillation objectives. On CIFAR-10, direct measurements show an 8.09% reduction in relative gradient variance and an 8.80% increase in gradient signal-to-noise ratio. Resource-constrained runs reach FID values of 1.475 for unconditional and 1.380 for conditional generation. These results show that EL is a lightweight and broadly compatible auxiliary objective for improving diffusion distillation under limited computational resources.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7429770","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T15:50:21Z","timestamp":1788796221000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/nextcomp70574.2026.11707918","title":["An Empirical Study of Synthetic Data Augmentation for Few-Shot Text Classification Using Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Ultra-Lightweight Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Aliya Bibi Hanaa","family":"Jaumeer","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University Of Mauritius","place":["Reduit, Mauritius"],"department":["Department of Information and Communication Technologies"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vidasha","family":"Ramnarain-Seetohul","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University Of Mauritius","place":["Reduit, Mauritius"],"department":["Department of Information and Communication Technologies"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/nextcomp70574.2026.11707918","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]],"date-time":"2026-10-02T19:11:57Z","timestamp":1790968317000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11707824\/11707894\/11707918.pdf?arnumber=11707918","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7465228","title":["Multi-Teacher Contrastive Distillation for Lightweight Plant Disease Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Liya","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yiwei","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaojun","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chunzhong","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2466-5990","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Changmiao","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jianpeng","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Lightweight deep models offer a promising avenue for real-time plant-disease recognition on edge devices, yet their accuracy often degrades under stringent compute budgets. We propose CropKD, a resource-aware classification framework that synergistically integrates three neural-computing components: (i) a lightweight multi-scale feature fusion module (LMFF) that enriches spatial representation via parallel depth-wise convolutions and channel-wise attention; (ii) a multi-teacher adaptive distillation strategy (MTAD) that dynamically fuses complementary knowledge from convolutional and vision-transformer teachers; and (iii) a contrastive knowledge distillation loss (CKD) that tightens intra-class embeddings while enlarging inter-class margins. Extensive experiments on FGVC8, BARI-Sunflower and DiaMOS show that CropKD achieves state-of-the-art accuracies of 94.5\\%, 98.99\\% and 94.11\\%, respectively, while retaining only 4.5 M parameters, 0.6 GFLOPs, 2.8 GB RAM and 95 W power. On a Jetson Nano it runs at 36 FPS, demonstrating real-time deployability under true field conditions. The results validate CropKD as a generic recipe for compressing heavy vision transformers into ultra-light edge-intelligence models, and provide a practical tool for sustainable smart agriculture. Code, pretrained weights and splits will be made publicly available.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7465228","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,15]],"date-time":"2026-09-15T13:25:46Z","timestamp":1789478746000}},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Brain tumor classification from magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) remains challenging due to subtle inter-class differences, imaging variability, and uncertainty in model predictions. Although deep learning models achieve strong performance, their complexity limits deployment in resource-constrained settings, making knowledge distillation a practical approach for transferring knowledge to lightweight student models. However, conventional distillation methods typically assume uniform teacher reliability and often overlook semantic structure and prediction reliability. To address this limitation, we propose a prototype-guided semantic transferability framework that selectively transfers knowledge based on predictive confidence and alignment with class prototypes in an embedding space. The method introduces a prototype memory to capture class-level structure and formulates a transferability score to adaptively weight the distillation process. Experiments demonstrate consistent improvements in classification performance, calibration, robustness, and cross-dataset generalization while preserving the efficiency of the student model. Ablation studies, statistical analysis, and feature space visualizations further support the effectiveness of the proposed components. Overall, the proposed framework aims to enhance knowledge distillation by incorporating semantic structure and uncertainty, leading to more reliable and discriminative representations without additional inference overhead.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1038\/s41598-026-72951-8","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T15:54:01Z","timestamp":1790265241000},"title":["Prototype-guided semantic transferability for reliable knowledge distillation in brain tumor MRI classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2534-8623","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Fariba Farhadi","family":"Birgani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.nature.com\/articles\/s41598-026-72951-8","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41598-026-72951-8","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Hallucinations in large language models (LLMs) remain a critical barrier to the safe deployment of AI-generated text in high-stakes domains. Existing detection methods typically rely on auxiliary LLMs, reference-based metrics, or human evaluation\u2014approaches that are computationally expensive, opaque, and difficult to audit. This paper presents the SAS Multimetric Tribunal, a lightweight, fully interpretable structural classification system that evaluates (source, response) pairs across seven detection modules using a cascading multiplicative penalty strategy. The tribunal partitions the output space into three structural zones\u2014Structural Collapse (F-S), Recoverable Rupture (B), and Coherent (A)\u2014governed by two thresholds:\u00a0\\(\\kappa_{D} = 0.56\\)\u00a0(coherence boundary, registered TAD EX-2026-18792778) and\u00a0\\(\\kappa_{R} = 0.15\\)\u00a0(collapse boundary, selected via entropy maximization of the zone distribution).\n\nOver an expanded benchmark of\u00a0\\(n = {1,800}\\)\u00a0pairs (600 HaluEval-Dialogue + 600 HaluEval-QA + 600 TruthfulQA), each evaluated under an A\\(\\rightarrow\\)A sanity check and an A\\(\\rightarrow\\)B hallucination condition, the tribunal achieves\u00a0\\(\\text{F1} = {98.99\\%}\\), Precision\u00a0\\(= {100.00\\%}\\)\u00a0(zero false positives under the sanity check), Recall\u00a0\\(= {98.00\\%}\\), and 36 false negatives (range across sampling seeds: F1 98.99\u201399.02%, FN 35\u201336). CRUCIALLY, WE DOCUMENT THAT 100% OF ERRORS (36\/36) CONCENTRATE IN TRUTHFULQA AND EXHIBIT A PREDICTABLE STRUCTURAL PATTERN: HIGH-LEXICAL-SIMILARITY FACTUAL HALLUCINATIONS THAT TRIGGER NO SPECIALIZED MODULE, LEAVING LEXICAL_BASELINE_SCORE AS THE SOLE DISCRIMINATOR. We treat these 36 false negatives not as noise but as the _documentable failure mode_ of the current lexical baseline against stealth hallucinations.\n\nWe demonstrate that cascading multiplicative penalty preserves per-module signal integrity better than weighted averaging, and we introduce SourceTargetGuard, a novel module for detecting critical-entity mutation under high lexical overlap. We explicitly caution that the reported metrics constitute an UPPER BOUND UNDER A TAUTOLOGICAL SANITY CHECK (A\\(\\rightarrow\\)A); the real negative control (A\\(\\rightarrow\\)C valid paraphrase), executed in this revision, confirms a 99.2% false-positive rate on paraphrases, which we document as the primary open limitation. This revised version additionally reports a FORENSIC AUDIT OF THE METRICS PUBLISHED IN THE PREVIOUS MANUSCRIPT VERSION: we could not initially locate a validation artifact matching the previously reported numbers, re-executed the validation under a fully documented and seeded protocol, and confirm that the earlier metrics were reproducible (not fabricated) but had been insufficiently documented at the time \u2014 a distinction we consider important enough to report in full rather than silently correct. This revision integrates a systematic Known Limitations section, articulates the methodological contributions, and corrects the documentation inconsistencies identified during peer review and internal audit.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.32388\/t420iv.3","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T08:50:28Z","timestamp":1790239828000},"title":["SAS Multimetric Tribunal: Structural Classification of Hallucination in AI-Generated Text"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-0916-3150","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Gonzalo","family":"Durante","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/T420IV.3\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"unspecified"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/T420IV.3\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/t420iv.3","relation":{"is-version-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.32388\/T420IV.2","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7454899","title":["HHRA: Hyperbolic Hierarchical Relation Alignment for Hierarchical Text Classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-6996-7292","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Jian","family":"Sun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2179-3874","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"yuan","family":"yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Hierarchical text classification assigns labels to documents according to a predefined label taxonomy. Existing methods often rely on shared document representations and static label structures, which may cause semantic interference among labels and fail to capture instance-specific hierarchical relationships. To address these limitations, we propose HHRA, a Hyperbolic Hierarchical Relation Alignment model. HHRA learns label-specific representations by separating primary discriminative features from complementary semantic information. It then maps label-specific text representations and label semantic representations into a Lorentz hyperbolic space, where hierarchy-aware contrastive learning is performed using positive, sibling, descendant, and random labels. The resulting text-conditioned relation priors are combined with the global label hierarchy to guide hierarchical feature re-aggregation. Experiments on four benchmark datasets, WOS, RCV1-V2, BGC, and NYT,\u00a0show that HHRA achieves the highest reported overall performance among the compared methods. In particular, it obtains Micro-F1 and Macro-F1 scores of 82.72 and 74.70 on NYT, respectively. Ablation studies further demonstrate the effectiveness of label-specific representation learning, hyperbolic relation alignment, and dynamic hierarchical re-aggregation. These results indicate that HHRA is particularly effective for hierarchical text classification with deep taxonomies and complex label paths.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7454899","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,13]],"date-time":"2026-09-13T14:41:48Z","timestamp":1789310508000}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.neucom.2026.135164","title":["DOR-vul: Cross-modal reasoning distillation for efficient vulnerability recognition"],"author":[{"given":"Amirreza","family":"Vafaei Moghadam","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7288-8112","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Davar","family":"Giveki","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.neucom.2026.135164","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,18]],"date-time":"2026-09-18T23:29:13Z","timestamp":1789774153000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0925231226025622?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0925231226025622?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.66857\/kjm9-a49f","title":["Energy-Efficient NLP Through Tiny-Model Distillation, Pruning, and Quantized Inference\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t| Kashf Journal of Multidisciplinary Research"],"author":[{"given":"Author","family":"Contributor","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.66857\/kjm9-a49f","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T10:34:15Z","timestamp":1788777255000}},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s00530-026-02636-9","title":["Semantic-conditioned temporal structure distillation for audio-text retrieval"],"author":[{"given":"Weijie","family":"Luo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaohan","family":"Guan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xichang","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jiafeng","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s00530-026-02636-9","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,6]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,6]],"date-time":"2026-09-06T09:07:24Z","timestamp":1788685644000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s00530-026-02636-9.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/article\/10.1007\/s00530-026-02636-9","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s00530-026-02636-9.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.patcog.2026.114994","title":["TeD-Loc: Text distillation for weakly supervised object localization"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8066-6907","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shakeeb","family":"Murtaza","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Soufiane","family":"Belharbi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alexis","family":"Guichemerre","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Marco","family":"Pedersoli","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Eric","family":"Granger","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.patcog.2026.114994","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,3]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T23:15:43Z","timestamp":1790205343000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0031320326019588?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0031320326019588?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7602593","title":["Cross-Modal Relation Preservation Knowledge Distillation from VLMs for Fine-Grained Visual Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Mengxi","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shali","family":"Yin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9781-8954","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Jun","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6391-7527","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Peirong","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yanhui","family":"Gu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Knowledge distillation has emerged as an effective approach to distill large-scale Vision-Language Models (VLMs) into lightweight networks under resource-constrained settings. However, existing VLM distillation methods typically transfer knowledge by matching output probabilities, requiring the student model to perform point-wise alignment with the teacher&amp;apos;s predictive distribution, which may fail to fully exploit the rich relational information embedded in VLM visual-language similarities. To address this issue, we propose Cross-Modal Relational Preservation Knowledge Distillation(CMRP-KD), which directly integrates the multimodal knowledge of VLMs into a compact model during training and shifts the distillation objective from probability matching to relational structure preservation. Specifically, CMRP-KD models cross-modal relations from two complementary levels of the VLM visual-language similarity matrix, and employs Pearson correlation-based constraints to preserve the relative relational structure between the teacher and student, thereby enabling more effective transfer of cross-modal knowledge. Extensive experiments on multiple fine-grained image classification benchmarks demonstrate that CMRP-KD generally outperforms existing VLM distillation methods, improving classification accuracy while maintaining high inference efficiency without introducing any additional computational overhead during inference. The code is available at https:\/\/github.com\/WMX1007\/CMRP-KD.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7602593","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T16:47:37Z","timestamp":1791823657000}},{"DOI":"10.32388\/7z13rm","title":["Review of: \"SAS Multimetric Tribunal: Structural Classification of Hallucination in AI-Generated Text\""],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9120-7298","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Evgenii","family":"Arsentev","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/7z13rm","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T16:00:04Z","timestamp":1790179204000},"relation":{"is-review-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.32388\/T420IV.2","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/7Z13RM\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"unspecified"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/7Z13RM\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.32388\/9t9p58","title":["Review of: \"SAS Multimetric Tribunal: Structural Classification of Hallucination in AI-Generated Text\""],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9120-7298","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Evgenii","family":"Arsentev","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/9t9p58","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]],"date-time":"2026-09-25T09:34:39Z","timestamp":1790328879000},"relation":{"is-review-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.32388\/T420IV.3","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/9T9P58\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"unspecified"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/9T9P58\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.32388\/ey8ww3","title":["Review of: \"SAS Multimetric Tribunal: Structural Classification of Hallucination in AI-Generated Text\""],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4355-1393","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Vipula","family":"Rawte","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/ey8ww3","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T10:13:03Z","timestamp":1788948783000},"relation":{"is-review-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.32388\/T420IV.2","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/EY8WW3\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"unspecified"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/EY8WW3\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7471312","title":["How Evaluation Splits Reshape Conclusions in Short-Text Field Classification: A Case Study on Educational Data Domain Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Hongxu","family":"Jiang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinan","family":"Shen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Junpeng","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Data classification and grading regulations require organizations to assign field-level semantic categories to structured data before sensitivity assessment, but short, context-poor field names make this nontrivial. We design and evaluate a character\u2013word\u2013sentence fusion architecture on a standardstraceable Chinese educational-field corpus (162 concepts drawn from seven Ministry-of-Education standards) under two evaluation protocols. Under aconventional sample-level split, character-only and word-only branches alone already approach ceiling performance (macro-F1 0.98 and 0.99), because the same concept\u2019s naming variants recur across training and test and these fromscratch embeddings memorize surface patterns rather than learn transferable semantics. Under a concept-level held-out split, where no test-set concept\u2019s name appears in training, the ranking reverses: over six partitions, characterand word-level branches degrade to mean macro-F1 0.38\u20130.39, while every combination including the pretrained sentence encoder scores substantially higher (0.51\u20130.56), a clean separation by whether the sentence branch is included. Enriching the input with the field\u2019s textual explanation, intended to help weak-semantics fields, achieves a leaked perfect score under the samplelevel split but is consistently worse than the plain input under concept-level holdout, across all six partitions. A confirmatory case study on 142 real fields from a Chinese university reproduces the same leaked-perfect-score failure mode and shows that a real deployment can be too table-scarce to construct the recommended held-out check. These findings indicate that sample-level splits on field-classification corpora with repeated underlying concepts can substantially overstate generalization, and we argue that concept-level (or table-level) held-out evaluation should be reported alongside conventional splits for this task family.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7471312","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T13:50:31Z","timestamp":1789566631000}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7467164","title":["Rafale: Towards the Efficient Resource-Aware Federated Knowledge Distillation on Heterogeneous Clients"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8258-377X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Harsh","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6205-4096","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Debasis","family":"Das","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-8424-2474","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Gozzal","family":"Eshniyazova","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5285-3775","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Doston","family":"Khasanov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5206-635X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Halimjon","family":"Khujamatov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Knowledge distillation is an highly effective machine learning technique for transferring knowledge from a large model, i.e., teacher, to a smaller model, i.e., student, improving model efficiency without sacrificing performance. This paper proposes a novel resource-aware federated learning framework Rafale that incorporates an adaptive client clustering mechanism based on computational resource availability and model complexity. High-resourceb clients train complex teacher models, and low-resource clients leverage distilled knowledge from teachers to train lightweight student models. Further, to optimize the aggregation process by introducing a clustering-based weightsharing mechanism that minimizes communication overhead while maintaining model accuracy. In this paper, a revolutionary Rafale framework that greatly reduces communication cost and separates model training from architectural constraints is proposed. To increase scalability and robustness, the suggested method makes use of resource-aware client selection, adaptive information sharing, and effective distillation techniques. In comparison to state-of-the-art FL approaches, experimental data show that RA-FKD delivers greater accuracy, decreases communication costs by up to 76%, and improves system efficiency. A cosine similarity loss KDC is introduced in knowledge distillation to improve the alignment between teacher and student models, enhancing knowledge transfer. Experimental evaluations on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 datasets demonstrate that proposed framework achieving student test accuracy of 91.15% with reduction of 76.08% communication cost.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7467164","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,15]],"date-time":"2026-09-15T18:24:32Z","timestamp":1789496672000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Background\/Objectives: Class imbalance and probabilistic prediction quality are important considerations in lightweight retinal classification. We evaluated whether knowledge distillation (KD) from a ConvNeXtV2-Base teacher improved an EfficientNet-B0 student in nine-class MultiEYE fundus classification. Methods: Exact-content screening quarantined 131 of 58,036 records because of exact-content duplication or label conflicts. Six prespecified seeds were evaluated in no-KD, label-smoothing, and KD arms (18 runs) under matched training conditions. Within the controlled experiment, TEST data were not used for training, hyperparameter tuning, checkpoint or model selection, or protocol modification. The primary endpoint was the KD\u2212no-KD macro-F1 difference on decontaminated TEST (n = 11,573), assessed by paired image-level bootstrap (10,000 replicates). Grad-CAM and Grad-CAM++ were examined qualitatively using one deterministically selected case per class and six-seed consensus maps. Results: Mean macro-F1 was 0.593, 0.597, and 0.633 for no-KD, label smoothing, and KD. KD exceeded no-KD by 0.039 (95% CI, 0.025\u20130.053; p &lt; 0.001), with positive paired differences in all six seeds, and label smoothing by 0.036 (Holm-adjusted p &lt; 0.001); label smoothing did not differ from no-KD (Holm-adjusted p = 0.554). Class-wise F1 differences were non-negative across all nine classes, with six remaining significant after correction for multiple comparisons. The Brier score difference was \u22120.077 (95% CI, \u22120.081 to \u22120.072; Holm-adjusted p &lt; 0.001), while ECE decreased descriptively from 0.171 to 0.084. Nine-class Grad-CAM and direct Grad-CAM++ consensus maps enabled qualitative no-KD versus KD comparison without establishing lesion-localization superiority. Conclusions: KD improved macro-F1 and lowered the Brier score while retaining the same EfficientNet-B0 student at inference. The evaluated label-smoothing configuration did not reproduce the macro-F1 gain; clinical superiority and external generalizability remain unestablished.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/diagnostics16182945","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T13:29:29Z","timestamp":1789133369000},"title":["Knowledge Distillation and Explainability Analysis for Lightweight Retinal Disease Classification Using MultiEYE Fundus Images"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3210-5061","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"G\u00fcl\u015fen","family":"T\u00fcrker","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Engineering, Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences, Suleyman Demirel University, 32260 Isparta, Turkey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"formal-analysis","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"data-curation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"visualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"project-administration","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6701-363X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Arif","family":"Koyun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Engineering, Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences, Suleyman Demirel University, 32260 Isparta, Turkey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"formal-analysis","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"data-curation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"project-administration","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"visualization","vocabulary":"credit"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2075-4418\/16\/18\/2945\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/diagnostics16182945","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.patcog.2026.115001","title":["Progressive latent calibration for stable score distillation in high-fidelity text-to-3D generation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-7905-0045","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Gyojin","family":"Han","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-7252-038X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jiwan","family":"Hur","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7174-7932","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Junmo","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.patcog.2026.115001","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,3]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]],"date-time":"2026-09-25T23:12:05Z","timestamp":1790377925000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0031320326019655?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0031320326019655?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tensymp68909.2026.11704053","title":["KALA-Net: Lightweight, Data-Efficient Knowledge Distillation Framework for Glaucoma Diagnosis"],"author":[{"given":"Pratiksha","family":"Gawas","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"National Institute of Technology Karnataka","place":["Mangalore, India"],"department":["Dept. of Information Technology"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sowmya Kamath","family":"Shevgoor","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Institute of Technology Karnataka","place":["Mangalore, India"],"department":["Dept. of Information Technology"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tensymp68909.2026.11704053","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:40:03Z","timestamp":1790624403000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11703968\/11703969\/11704053.pdf?arnumber=11704053","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.54254\/2755-2721\/2026.36457","title":["Cross-Resolution Knowledge Distillation for Low-Compute Fine-Grained Bird Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Chenqi","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing Normal-Hong Kong Baptist University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Fine-grained visual classification relies on subtle local cues and is highly sensitive to input resolution, yet practical deployment often constrains image size and inference cost. Existing low-resolution recognition methods often depend on additional network structures or complex training modules, limiting deployment simplicity. This study evaluates a simple cross-resolution knowledge distillation (KD) strategy under a fixed low-compute constraint. The teacher receives higher-resolution inputs to provide richer class-discriminative visual knowledge, whereas the student is trained at 160\u00d7160 and is the only model retained for deployment. Both networks use ResNet-18, allowing resolution-aware supervision to be studied without changing the inference architecture. On the CUB-200-2011 dataset, the 160\u00d7160 baseline achieved 66.01\u00b10.53% test accuracy. The 224\u2192160 KD configuration improved accuracy to 70.15\u00b10.63%, while the final 384\u2192160 configuration with T = 4 , \u03b1 = 0.75 , and a 30-epoch cosine schedule reached 71.60\u00b10.68% accuracy and 71.56\u00b10.62% macro-F1. The results also show that a stronger teacher does not automatically produce a stronger student: transfer quality depends on the balance between teacher supervision strength and student optimization. These findings support cross-resolution KD as a practical way to recover fine-grained information during training while preserving low-resolution inference cost.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54254\/2755-2721\/2026.36457","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]],"date-time":"2026-09-01T03:39:41Z","timestamp":1788233981000}},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-032-38224-5_28","title":["LRMIL: Efficient Low-Resolution Multiple Instance Learning via\u00a0High-Resolution Knowledge Distillation for\u00a0Whole Slide Image Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Yonghan","family":"Shin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Won-Ki","family":"Jeong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-032-38224-5_28","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T21:16:43Z","timestamp":1790284603000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/978-3-032-38224-5_28","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/access.2026.3738308","title":["DisagreementNet: Lightweight Multi-Teacher Distillation for Retinal OCT Classification\u2014A Leakage-Aware Evaluation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-4629-458X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Miit","family":"Daga","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Vellore Institute of Technology","place":["Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8287-9690","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Swarna Priya","family":"Ramu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Vellore Institute of Technology","place":["Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/access.2026.3738308","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T19:08:09Z","timestamp":1790708889000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/6287639\/11323511\/11715661.pdf?arnumber=11715661","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/imsa70415.2026.11700262","title":["Cross-Architecture Knowledge Distillation for Efficient Autoencoder-Based Intrusion Detection"],"author":[{"given":"Youssef","family":"Abdulghany","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"MSA University","place":["Giza, Egypt"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ayad","family":"Zewail","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"MSA University","place":["Giza, Egypt"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ahmed","family":"El-Shamy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"MSA University","place":["Giza, Egypt"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/imsa70415.2026.11700262","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:14:38Z","timestamp":1790622878000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11700243\/11700244\/11700262.pdf?arnumber=11700262","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.32604\/cmc.2026.087209","title":["Enhancing Biomedical Multi-Label Text Classification via Topic-Based Text Representation"],"author":[{"given":"Oyku Berfin","family":"Mercan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nezihe","family":"Turhan Turan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Aytu\u011f","family":"Onan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32604\/cmc.2026.087209","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T09:13:43Z","timestamp":1788772423000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/cdn.techscience.cn\/files\/cmc\/2026\/TSP_CMC-89-2\/TSP_CMC_87209\/TSP_CMC_87209.pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.solener.2026.115122","title":["Dual teacher multiscale distillation for lightweight solar cell defect classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-1005-4229","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hasnain","family":"Hyder","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4759-0138","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yong-Woon","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chulung","family":"Kang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yung-Cheol","family":"Byun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.solener.2026.115122","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T12:39:00Z","timestamp":1791808740000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0038092X2600811X?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0038092X2600811X?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.32388\/cgu3sm","title":["Review of: \"Efficient Pruning of Text-to-Image Models: Insights from Pruning Stable Diffusion\""],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9120-7298","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Evgenii","family":"Arsentev","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/cgu3sm","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T08:04:06Z","timestamp":1791273846000},"relation":{"is-review-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.32388\/IOO0D9","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/CGU3SM\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"unspecified"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/CGU3SM\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7434454","title":["BioScaleML: A Lakehouse-Based Framework for,Large-Scale Biomedical Text Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Kenneth  T.","family":"He","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Max  M.","family":"He","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Objective: The exponential growth of unstructured biomedical text across scientific literature, clinical narratives, and registries requires scalable, privacy-conscious informatics infrastructure. Existing solutions often rely heavily on managed cloud ecosystems, creating data governance, cost, and vendor lock-in challenges. This study presents BioScaleML, an open-source, local Lakehouse architecture designed to integrate end-to-end biomedical data management, scalable distributed processing, and reproducible machine learning (ML) pipelines within a controlled on-premises environment.,Methods: BioScaleML was engineered by coupling Apache Spark for distributed computation, Delta Lake for ACID-compliant transactional storage and schema enforcement, Hadoop for local persistence, and a PostgreSQL-backed Hive metastore for unified metadata management. The framework was evaluated across two curated biomedical text corpora totaling 49,118 documents (19,681 PubMed-style abstracts and 29,437 full-text article sections) annotated across major oncological categories (breast, lung, and prostate). Standardized Extract-Transform-Load (ETL) pipelines engineered TF-IDF feature representations, feeding reusable ML pipelines trained on local hardware.,Results: The integrated architecture enabled deterministic dataset versioning, robust schema governance, and end-to-end workflow execution on commodity infrastructure without cloud reliance. Baseline classification models\u2014including Multinomial Logistic Regression, Linear Support Vector Machines, and Multinomial Naive Bayes\u2014achieved consistent high-throughput classification across both corpora (reaching up to 90.6% accuracy), demonstrating that enterprise-grade data management and NLP processing can be achieved locally.,Conclusion: BioScaleML provides a practical, cost-effective blueprint for healthcare and research institutions seeking reproducible biomedical text analytics under strict data sovereignty constraints. By establishing a transaction-aware foundation, BioScaleML provides an extensible framework for scaling local informatics pipelines to advanced domain-specific language models and multi-modal clinical datasets.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7434454","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,14]],"date-time":"2026-09-14T07:14:09Z","timestamp":1789370049000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/cisat71030.2026.11682442","title":["Enhancing Lightweight Models for Edge-Device Mineral Classification through Multi-Strategy Knowledge Distillation"],"author":[{"given":"Yi","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Xi'an Jiaotong University","place":["Xi'an, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/cisat71030.2026.11682442","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,10]],"date-time":"2026-09-10T19:06:31Z","timestamp":1789067191000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11682029\/11682043\/11682442.pdf?arnumber=11682442","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.3390\/engproc2026154055","title":["An Interactive Web Platform for Analyzing the Impact of Hyperparameters in Text Classification Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2087-4245","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Daniela","family":"Petrova","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Computer Sciences and Automation, Technical University of Varna, 9010 Varna, Bulgaria"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/engproc2026154055","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T17:07:19Z","timestamp":1788800839000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2673-4591\/154\/1\/55\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.61686\/gafdq92929","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.61686\/gafdq92929","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T13:00:16Z","timestamp":1788786016000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/taslpro.2026.3724417\/mm1","title":["LLCE-SQL: Data-Efficient Text-to-SQL Training via Logic-Linking Driven Collaborative Evolution_supp1-3724417.pdf"],"author":[{"given":"Yanfei","family":"Sun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/taslpro.2026.3724417\/mm1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]],"date-time":"2026-09-01T19:12:51Z","timestamp":1788289971000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.1109\/TASLPRO.2026.3724417","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Choosing between zero-shot inference and supervised fine-tuning is often a label-budget decision: practitioners want to know when additional supervision will make adaptation worthwhile. Existing comparisons usually determine this threshold only after training models across multiple data budgets, leaving no low-cost way to anticipate it. We test whether class separability in frozen sentence-embedding spaces can serve as a training-free, although label-dependent, predictor of this break-even. Because separability itself requires class labels, the experiment deliberately evaluates a strong-form geometric signal rather than an annotation-free deployment rule. In a prospectively prespecified study spanning 35 public English-language text-classification tasks and three sentence encoders, we related pre-adaptation separability to the label budget at which a supervised Transformer classifier first exceeded a fixed instruction-tuned zero-shot comparator. Silhouette-based separability did not satisfy the prespecified inferential and practical-utility criteria, and an alternative separability measure produced a different directional pattern. Many tasks also failed to reach break-even within the tested budget, producing substantial right censoring. Censoring-aware, heteroskedasticity-robust, and influence-oriented post hoc analyses did not materially change the interpretation. Frozen embedding separability alone is therefore not a sufficiently stable estimator of fine-tuning break-even in this comparison regime. These findings place an empirical boundary on a plausible geometric heuristic and suggest that useful forecasts will likely require comparator-aware information, local boundary structure, or early adaptation dynamics.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10975833\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,21]],"date-time":"2026-09-21T10:22:57Z","timestamp":1789986177000},"title":["Forecasting Fine-Tuning Break-Even Label Budgets in Text Classification from Frozen Embedding Geometry"],"author":[{"given":"Emin Talip","family":"Demirkiran","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Eskisehir Technical University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-10975833\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-10975833\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10975833\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,21]]}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Classifying scanned pages by document type is a routing step in enterprisedocument processing. Layout-aware models reach high accuracy but rely onlarge-scale document pretraining and full fine-tuning, and seldom expose why adecision was made. We study a deliberately lightweight alternative, GLaDViT,which keeps CLIP and BERT encoders frozen, derives a layout descriptor fromTesseract hOCR word boxes, and trains only a gated fusion head. Because theclassifier is linear in the gated sum of modality embeddings, every class logitdecomposes exactly into per-modality terms, which yields an attribution that isfaithful to the computation by construction, unlike the gate weights alone. Ona label-corrected release of Tobacco-3482 (2737 pages, fixed 2186\/272\/279split), GLaDViT reaches \\((93.7\\pm0.4%)\\) accuracy over three seeds (95% Wilsoninterval 90.2--96.0%), against 87.7% for the best single modality; addinglayout to vision and text contributes 2.8 accuracy points and gating a further1.1. The layout stream nevertheless receives at most about 10% of the gatemass, indicating that gate weights are mixing coefficients rather thanimportance scores. A triple InfoNCE alignment of the three streams, used as awarm-up on frozen features, lowers accuracy to 90.3%; we analyse this negativeresult. Code and hOCR tooling will be made publicly available.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11167180\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:33:03Z","timestamp":1791880383000},"title":["GLaDViT: interpretable gated fusion of frozen vision, text and layout features for document image classification"],"author":[{"given":"Murat","family":"Simsek","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Ostim Technical University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11167180\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11167180\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11167180\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/access.2026.3735600","title":["Structural and Statistical Audio Texture Knowledge Distillation for Acoustic Classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-4278-5784","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jarin","family":"Ritu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Texas A&M University","place":["College Station, TX, USA"],"department":["Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7558-1706","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Amirmohammad","family":"Mohammadi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Texas A&M University","place":["College Station, TX, USA"],"department":["Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Davelle","family":"Carreiro","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology Lincoln Laboratory","place":["Lexington, MA, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alexandra Van","family":"Dine","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology Lincoln Laboratory","place":["Lexington, MA, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7861-7789","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Joshua","family":"Peeples","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Texas A&M University","place":["College Station, TX, USA"],"department":["Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/access.2026.3735600","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T19:19:05Z","timestamp":1790191145000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/6287639\/11323511\/11700448.pdf?arnumber=11700448","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.knosys.2026.116989","title":["SSKD-Depth: Spectral\u2013semantic Knowledge Distillation for efficient monocular depth estimation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-1545-1346","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Guangyi","family":"Ji","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-2273-8447","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaoxi","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1867-6064","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tao","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.knosys.2026.116989","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T23:26:36Z","timestamp":1788823596000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0950705126017156?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0950705126017156?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.patcog.2026.115039","title":["A dynamic fusion enhanced trajectory distillation framework for multi-view classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-3316-8530","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hongyu","family":"Yao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Binlu","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaobin","family":"Hong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yanchao","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9336-1013","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaoli","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.patcog.2026.115039","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,3]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]],"date-time":"2026-09-30T15:42:56Z","timestamp":1790782976000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0031320326020030?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0031320326020030?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Text classification with few labeled examples requires models that can effectively exploit unlabeled data while remaining lightweight. Graph-based approaches address this by modeling documents through their relations, but existing graph contrastive methods for text often rely on augmentations that alter semantic structure and are affected by unreliable false negatives. We propose Multi-View Dual-Contrastive Graph Learning (MDCGA), a framework that learns a shared document representation by integrating lexical, semantic, and keyword-based relational signals through enhanced dual contrastive objectives designed to reduce the impact of false negatives. Experiments on MR, Ohsumed, AG News, and R8 show that MDCGA achieves accuracies of 85.71%, 70.49%, 92.76%, and 97.40%, respectively. The approach demonstrates robustness in very low labeled settings, uses relatively few parameters, and shows limited sensitivity to hyperparameter choices.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9498262\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]],"date-time":"2026-09-01T02:01:41Z","timestamp":1788228101000},"title":["Multi-View Dual-Contrastive Graph Learning with Adaptive Agreement for Semi-Supervised Text Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Ibtissam","family":"Youb","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ISCAE Group"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mohamed","family":"Hamlich","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Hassan II"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-9498262\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-9498262\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9498262\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7550121","title":["Unified Attention for Efficient Image Classification and Captioning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0397-3962","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Zanyar","family":"Zohourianshahzadi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Terrance  E.","family":"Boult","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Transformers are the standard for visual representation learning and multimodal tasks like image captioning, but conventional multi-head attention relies on separate linear projections for queries, keys, and values, causing parameter redundancy and high computational cost. We propose Unified Attention, a parameter-efficient mechanism that reuses a single linear transformation across multiple attention passes. We instantiate this in Unified Vision Transformers (UViT) for image classification and Unified Transformers (UTR) for image captioning. Our approach reduces parameters per attention block by up to 75% while preserving essential structural dependencies and capturing consistent mappings between inputs and outputs. Experiments show that UTR improves image captioning metrics across multiple datasets, while UViT achieves competitive ImageNet top-1 accuracy of 83% with fewer parameters than baseline ViT. Unified Attention offers both a mapping-based interpretation of attention and a practical strategy for designing compact, efficient transformers for vision and multimodal tasks. The code for our experiments will be available on github upon acceptance.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7550121","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]],"date-time":"2026-10-02T14:04:48Z","timestamp":1790949888000}},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-032-39898-7_16","title":["Efficient Text Graph Classification via\u00a0Graph Condensation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-6994-1510","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ren\u00e9 Vieira","family":"Santin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-7225-7974","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"N\u00edcolas Roque","family":"dos Santos","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4735-0337","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Diego","family":"Minatel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5475-1382","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Al\u00edpio","family":"Jorge","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5233-7639","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Solange Oliveira","family":"Rezende","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-032-39898-7_16","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T17:43:51Z","timestamp":1790703831000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/978-3-032-39898-7_16","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ccsb71176.2026.11716406","title":["KTCTPose: Efficient Coarse-to-Fine Human Pose Estimation via Knowledge-Token Self-Distillation"],"author":[{"given":"Guodong","family":"Zheng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"China Jiliang University","place":["Hangzhou, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiang","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China Jiliang University","place":["Hangzhou, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ccsb71176.2026.11716406","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T19:26:58Z","timestamp":1791314818000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11716289\/11716303\/11716406.pdf?arnumber=11716406","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/bigdia71496.2026.11684652","title":["Decision-Boundary-Aware Adversarial Distillation for Remote Sensing Scene Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Xu","family":"Guo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"USTC University of Science and Technology of China","place":["Hefei, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lyuzhou","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"USTC University of Science and Technology of China","place":["Hefei, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiangyu","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"USTC University of Science and Technology of China","place":["Hefei, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinwen","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"USTC University of Science and Technology of China","place":["Hefei, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/bigdia71496.2026.11684652","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T19:13:27Z","timestamp":1789586007000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11684546\/11684527\/11684652.pdf?arnumber=11684652","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Breast cancer is a highly heterogeneous disease at the histopathological and molecular levels, and histological grade is a key prognostic indicator reflecting tumor biology. Although Random Forest models effectively capture nonlinear patterns in high-dimensional gene expression data, the decision logic of large tree ensembles remains difficult to inspect directly. This study proposes a knowledge-distillation framework that transfers the probabilistic decision behavior of a Random Forest teacher model to a compact, rule-based student model for distinguishing low- from high-histological-grade breast tumors using 20,385 gene-expression features from 1892 METABRIC samples, with clinical information used for sample matching and histological-grade label definition. Root-to-leaf decision paths extracted from the teacher were converted into a binary rule-activation matrix, followed by proximal group sparsification and fidelity-constrained Top-K rule selection. For the principal 400-tree teacher configuration, the student retained 65.0 \u00b1 13.7 rules and achieved an ROC-AUC of 0.833 \u00b1 0.023 and an average precision of 0.843 \u00b1 0.019, while maintaining a raw teacher\u2013student Pearson fidelity of 0.9607 \u00b1 0.0112. As teacher capacity increased, the number of active decision paths increased substantially, whereas the selected rule budget remained within a comparatively narrow range and increased only modestly. The compact student also showed approximately 46% lower measured end-to-end inference latency and a 76% smaller serialized deployment size than the 400-tree teacher. Within the present METABRIC evaluation, these findings indicate that the structural complexity of a tree ensemble and the complexity required to approximate its probabilistic behavior need not scale proportionally, and that the proposed framework can provide a favorable trade-off among predictive performance, teacher fidelity, and deployment compactness.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/app16178574","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]],"date-time":"2026-09-01T10:32:18Z","timestamp":1788258738000},"title":["Fidelity-Constrained Rule-Based Knowledge Distillation of Random Forests for Compact Breast Cancer Grade Classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2288-3986","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mert","family":"B\u00fcy\u00fckdede","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Engineering, Istanbul Atlas University, 34408 Istanbul, T\u00fcrkiye"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"visualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-5725-0090","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Esma G\u00fclfem","family":"Akta\u015f","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Engineering, Istanbul Atlas University, 34408 Istanbul, T\u00fcrkiye"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"resources","vocabulary":"credit"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2076-3417\/16\/17\/8574\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/app16178574","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,28]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Multivariate time-series forecasting models often achieve improved prediction accuracy at the cost of increased model complexity and inference latency, limiting their deployment on resource-constrained computing platforms. In addition, lower parameter counts or theoretical computational costs do not necessarily lead to faster execution on actual hardware. To address this issue, we propose a Latency-Constrained Distillation and Dynamic Pruning (LCDP) framework for efficient multivariate time-series forecasting. LCDP introduces a latency-constrained adaptive optimization strategy that jointly considers forecasting loss, knowledge-distillation loss, local feature-alignment loss, and hardware inference latency during student-model training. Local feature-guided knowledge transfer is used to enhance the student model\u2019s ability to capture fine-grained temporal patterns, while deployment-oriented dynamic structural pruning progressively adjusts the group-wise sparsity pattern of the student model according to latency feedback from the target hardware while retaining the original dense layer dimensions. Experiments on the ETTh1, ETTm1, and ETTm2 datasets using two CPU platforms show that LCDP achieves competitive forecasting accuracy with a lightweight student inference backbone containing 7336 active parameters and requiring 50,848 dense multiply-accumulate operations. The saved student checkpoint additionally contains 2080 parameters in a training-time feature-alignment projection that is not invoked during inference. LCDP provides an effective balance between forecasting accuracy and practical inference efficiency, demonstrating its potential for low-latency multivariate time-series forecasting on CPU-based resource-constrained platforms.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/e28101104","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T06:16:26Z","timestamp":1791785786000},"title":["Efficient Multivariate Time-Series Forecasting via Latency-Constrained Distillation and Dynamic Pruning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8047-1010","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yuting","family":"Bai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"National Engineering Research Center for Agri-Product Quality Traceability Technology and Application, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"},{"name":"School of Computer and Artificial Intelligence, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xixian","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer and Artificial Intelligence, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0074-3467","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jianlei","family":"Kong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Engineering Research Center for Agri-Product Quality Traceability Technology and Application, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"},{"name":"School of Computer and Artificial Intelligence, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2230-0077","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xuebo","family":"Jin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Engineering Research Center for Agri-Product Quality Traceability Technology and Application, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"},{"name":"School of Computer and Artificial Intelligence, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tingli","family":"Su","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Engineering Research Center for Agri-Product Quality Traceability Technology and Application, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"},{"name":"School of Computer and Artificial Intelligence, Beijing Technology and Business University, Beijing 102488, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/1099-4300\/28\/10\/1104\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/e28101104","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7606545","title":["Sequential Shared-LoRA Adaptation of BERT for Text Classification: A Continual-Learning Baseline"],"author":[{"given":"M.  Dyva Sugnana","family":"Rao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"B Vishnu","family":"Vardhan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Fine-tuning a pre-trained language model separately for every classification task is costly in computation and storage, and scales poorly when tasks arrive over time. Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) reduces this cost, but whether a single shared adapter can serve several classification tasks trained one after another remains underexplored. We study a frozen BERT-base backbone with one shared LoRA adapter in its self-attention layers and lightweight task-specific heads, trained sequentially on three tasks derived from the aclImdb movie-review corpus: binary sentiment, a rating-bucket task derived from the 1\u201310 review ratings, and a heuristic five-class genre task built from keyword rules, since the corpus has no genre metadata. The adapter and heads together comprise 302,602 of 109,784,842 parameters (0.2756%), cutting estimated storage for three tasks by 66.5% relative to per-task full fine-tuning. Each task peaks immediately after its own training stage (sentiment 88.6%, rating 89.1% against a 50% majority baseline, genre 57.2%). Sequential training, however, causes measurable forgetting: sentiment accuracy falls monotonically to 81.7% (forgetting score 0.0697) and rating accuracy from 89.1% to 83.5% (0.0550). These single-run results show that a compact shared adapter can support several related tasks while exposing interference under sequential adaptation, and they provide a reference accuracy\u2013retention trade-off against which architecture-, regularization- and replay-based continual-learning mitigations can be evaluated.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7606545","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T12:44:26Z","timestamp":1791895466000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Hallucination-detection systems are usually judged by a single headline metric, computed under a benchmark design that determines what that metric can show. This paper\u2019s contribution is not a detection algorithm \u2014 structural coherence scoring is established \u2014 but a methodology for measuring exactly what a benchmark proves and what it does not, applied to the SAS Multimetric Tribunal: a seven-module, GPU-free structural classifier combining lexical, entity-mutation, curvature, and logical-inversion signals via a cascading multiplicative penalty (\\(\\kappa_{D} = 0.56\\),\u00a0\\(\\kappa_{R} = 0.15\\)).\n\nOn a stratified 1,800-pair benchmark (HaluEval-Dialogue, HaluEval-QA, TruthfulQA), the tribunal reaches F1 = 98.99% with zero false positives under an A\\(\\rightarrow\\)A identical-text sanity check \u2014 matched or exceeded by trivial baselines, including exact string matching (F1 = 100%). We report this explicitly: a benchmark that cannot fail on identical text cannot establish precision. Under a real negative control (R3: 127 valid paraphrase pairs), every method tested fails \u2014 the tribunal flags 126\/127 as hallucinations (specificity 0.8%), matched by simple lexical baselines. No lexical-overlap method in our comparison distinguishes a valid paraphrase from a hallucination.\n\nWe also report, as direct ablations rather than argument, that multiplicative combination outperforms weighted averaging of the same seven modules by 87.97 F1 points, and that SourceTargetGuard\u2019s measured contribution is 3 of 1,800 true positives (0.17% of recall). The tribunal\u2019s contribution is not a solved precision problem: it is an auditable, per-module architecture on which a semantic-equivalence guard can be added and measured \u2014 something a single-metric baseline offers no place to attach. This revision also reports a forensic audit distinguishing an earlier version\u2019s undocumented-but-reproducible metrics from a stale sub-count, corrected in response to independent peer review.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.32388\/t420iv.4","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T13:31:25Z","timestamp":1791293485000},"title":["SAS Multimetric Tribunal: Structural Classification of Hallucination in AI-Generated Text \u2013 What a Benchmark Design Can and Cannot Show"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-0916-3150","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Gonzalo","family":"Durante","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/T420IV.4\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"unspecified"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/T420IV.4\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/t420iv.4","relation":{"is-version-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.32388\/T420IV.3","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.patrec.2026.09.038","title":["GeoDistillGaze: A knowledge distillation framework for efficient gaze target prediction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-0387-0664","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tianzhong","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinhe","family":"Su","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Siyu","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.patrec.2026.09.038","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T15:18:14Z","timestamp":1790608694000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0167865526003533?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0167865526003533?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7526818","title":["ClozeCongruence: Human-Style Essay Classification vs. AI-Written Text via Multi-Horizon Semantic Infilling and Calibrated Stylometric Manifolds"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-7496-0767","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Debdip","family":"Bandyopadhyay","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Distinguishing authentic human-authored essays\n&lt;br&gt;\nfrom machine-generated prose remains an active challenge for\n&lt;br&gt;\neducational integrity, admissions authenticity, and digital forensics.\n&lt;br&gt;\nExisting zero-shot detectors either rely on inaccessible white-box\n&lt;br&gt;\ntoken logits (DetectGPT, Fast-DetectGPT) or penalize formal,\n&lt;br&gt;\nnon-native human writing styles through uncalibrated perplexity\n&lt;br&gt;\nmetrics. Furthermore, prior forensic benchmarks frequently\n&lt;br&gt;\nevaluate specialized technical preprints (e.g., ArXiv, PeerRead)\n&lt;br&gt;\nor truncate samples to 200 words, destroying macro-discursive\n&lt;br&gt;\nstructure. In this paper, we present ClozeCongruence, a controlled\n&lt;br&gt;\nforensic evaluation framework evaluated on a curated benchmark\n&lt;br&gt;\nof N = 6, 997 samples dedicated to full-length human-style essay\n&lt;br&gt;\nclassification vs. AI-written text (mean length \u2248 640 words, up\n&lt;br&gt;\nto 1,500 words).\n&lt;br&gt;\nOur methodology evaluates five core empirical questions under\n&lt;br&gt;\nzero-target-leakage protocols (verified via label permutation\n&lt;br&gt;\ntests yielding null AUROCs of 0.50 \u00b1 0.02). First, we enforce\n&lt;br&gt;\npairwise prompt matching across authentic multi-paragraph\n&lt;br&gt;\nreflective, explanatory, and encyclopedic essays and frontier LLM\n&lt;br&gt;\ngenerations. With N = 3, 499 verified human negative controls,\n&lt;br&gt;\nthe dataset satisfies the Rule of Three (N \u2265 3\/\u03b1) required\n&lt;br&gt;\nto calibrate False Positive Rates down to 0.1%. Second, on\n&lt;br&gt;\nclean in-domain full essays (N = 6, 000), zero-shot stylometric\n&lt;br&gt;\nscoring achieves an AUROC of 0.8547 [95% Bootstrap CI:\n&lt;br&gt;\n0.8443\u20130.8642] and AUPRC of 0.8520, yielding True Positive\n&lt;br&gt;\nRates of 43.27% at 5.0% FPR, 20.17% at 1.0% FPR, and\n&lt;br&gt;\n5.30% at 0.1% FPR (a 75-fold sensitivity gain over 200-word\n&lt;br&gt;\ntruncated baselines). When calibrated through a 5-fold cross-\n&lt;br&gt;\nvalidated supervised manifold ensemble, discrimination reaches\n&lt;br&gt;\nAUROC 0.9651 with 52.17% TPR at 1.0% FPR and 15.87%\n&lt;br&gt;\nTPR at 0.1% FPR. In isolated ablation testing, single-scalar\n&lt;br&gt;\nparagraph discourse flow variance alone achieves an AUROC\n&lt;br&gt;\nof 0.5343 (Cohen\u2019s d = 0.105), demonstrating that discourse\n&lt;br&gt;\nprogression is an integrative feature within multi-scale manifolds\n&lt;br&gt;\nrather than an isolated standalone classifier. Third, on the Full\n&lt;br&gt;\nRAID 11-Attack Adversarial Benchmark (N = 6, 000, Dugan et\n&lt;br&gt;\nal., ACL 2024), pre-inference canonicalization mitigates surface\n&lt;br&gt;\nattacks (homoglyph substitution recovers from 0.6793 \u2192 0.9649\n&lt;br&gt;\nAUROC, \u2206 = +0.2856, 62.4% TPR @ 1% FPR; clean baseline\n&lt;br&gt;\nmaintains 0.9799 AUROC), while establishing the empirical failure\n&lt;br&gt;\nboundary against 11B DIPPER neural paraphrasing (0.6956\n&lt;br&gt;\nAUROC, 13.2% TPR @ 1% FPR; Overall Benchmark AUROC\n&lt;br&gt;\n= 0.9459). Fourth, evaluating out-of-domain cross-domain and\n&lt;br&gt;\nzero-shot multilingual transfer on the M4 Benchmark (N = 4, 000\n&lt;br&gt;\nacross 8 strata and 6 languages) demonstrates performance\n&lt;br&gt;\non structured academic English (PeerRead AUROC = 0.9977,\n&lt;br&gt;\n92.0% TPR @ 1% FPR; Reddit AUROC = 0.8660) whilecharacterizing natural script attenuation across Arabic (0.8706),\n&lt;br&gt;\nGerman (0.8008), Chinese (0.7143), Urdu (0.7038), and Russian\n&lt;br&gt;\n(0.6700) without language-specific tuning (Full M4 AUROC =\n&lt;br&gt;\n0.8165). Fifth, addressing human-AI co-writing on the WildChat\n&lt;br&gt;\nChimeric Benchmark (N = 2, 000 documents, 9,995 paragraphs),\n&lt;br&gt;\nour sliding-window boundary localizer achieves 0.8979 paragraph\n&lt;br&gt;\nclassification AUROC, exact transition localization F1 of 0.6789\n&lt;br&gt;\n(\u00b10 paragraphs), and slack F1 of 0.7517 (\u00b11 paragraph), reaching\n&lt;br&gt;\n100.0% Precision and 0.8322 F1 on alternating co-authorship.\n&lt;br&gt;\nAll code, localizer weights, and curated benchmark datasets\n&lt;br&gt;\nare publicly released at https:\/\/huggingface.co\/datasets\/DebdipCS\/\n&lt;br&gt;\nhuman-vs-ai-essay-benchmark.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7526818","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T12:10:22Z","timestamp":1790597422000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper systematically compared the high performance \u03c00 model with the high efficiency SmolVLA model. Real machine evaluation revealed that \u03c0&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;\/sub&gt; relying on its powerful flow matching action of generator and huge VLM model number, precision lead in vertical task; While SmolVLA through lightweight attention head and asynchronous reasoning stack, on delay, power consumption and anti-jamming advantage is obvious. In order to integrate the advantages of the two models, we innovatively propose an action knowledge distillation method at the output layer to transfer the action strategy knowledge of the \u03c0&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;\/sub&gt; model to SmolVLA, so that it can obtain similar performance improvement to the former with negligible additional overhead. This study provides an effective performance enhancement scheme for the efficient and practical deployment of the robot VLA model.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1117\/12.3125783","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T16:57:14Z","timestamp":1791305834000},"title":["SmolVLA-Diff: efficient knowledge distillation for VLA models for vertical task deployment"],"author":[{"given":"Yuwei","family":"Qin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.spiedigitallibrary.org\/conference-proceedings-of-spie\/14353\/3125783\/SmolVLA-Diff--efficient-knowledge-distillation-for-VLA-models-for\/10.1117\/12.3125783.full","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1117\/12.3125783","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.eswa.2026.134741","title":["A Multi-Scale Semantic Perception Network with Multi-Relational Text Graphs for Text Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Chongxu","family":"Hu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-6819-0652","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaoyan","family":"Gongye","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-1568-1333","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhe","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jianlei","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Peng","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.eswa.2026.134741","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,14]],"date-time":"2026-10-14T23:58:32Z","timestamp":1792022312000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0957417426036456?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0957417426036456?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Hyperspectral image (HSI) classification is critical for remote sensing but faces challenges in balancing accuracy and inference efficiency. Existing graph-based knowledge distillation (KD) methods are confined to single geometric spaces, ignoring the hierarchical semantics of land-cover categories. Cross-geometry distillation compresses multiple geometries into a single Euclidean student, forcing one geometry to collapse into the other. In this paper, we propose Dual-Space Knowledge Distillation (DSKD), a novel dual-student dual-space KD framework integrating Euclidean (GCN) and Hyperbolic (HGCN) teachers to jointly train a native MLP student and a native HNN student. With cross-geometric feature bridging (CGFB) and output distribution cohesion (ODC), the two students mutually learn each other\u2019s complementary geometry, so DSKD captures complementary spatial-spectral and hierarchical features while enabling graph-free inference without message passing. Extensive experiments on four HSI datasets and four general graph benchmarks demonstrate that DSKD outperforms single-space distillation and single-student cross-geometry baselines in most settings, confirming its effectiveness and generalization capability across diverse graph-structured data.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/rs18183127","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T14:39:24Z","timestamp":1789137564000},"title":["Dual-Space Knowledge Distillation with Cross-Geometric Feature Interaction for Hyperspectral Image Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Ting","family":"Yuan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer and Electronic Information\/School of Artificial Intelligence, Nanjing Normal University, Nanjing 210023, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenzhu","family":"Yan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer and Electronic Information\/School of Artificial Intelligence, Nanjing Normal University, Nanjing 210023, China"},{"name":"School of Digital and Information Technology, Hangzhou Xiaoshan Technician College, Hangzhou 311200, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4761-4726","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Youqiang","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Internet of Things, Nanjing University of Posts and Telecommunications, Nanjing 210003, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sheng","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"AISpeech Co., Ltd., Suzhou 215123, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2072-4292\/18\/18\/3127\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/rs18183127","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.adhoc.2026.104406","title":["Multi-Level Contrastive Knowledge Distillation for Resource-efficient Visual Inference on Edge Devices"],"author":[{"given":"Ting","family":"Liang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lingyu","family":"Du","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2190-9937","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Guohao","family":"Lan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.adhoc.2026.104406","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,2]],"date-time":"2026-09-02T15:58:34Z","timestamp":1788364714000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S1570870526002726?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S1570870526002726?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014714600005024","title":["Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Medical Named Entity Recognition: An Empirical Study of LoRa on Biomedical Text"],"author":[{"given":"Vikashini","family":"S.","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science and Technology (AI & ML), Karunya Institute of Technology and Sciences, Coimbatore, Tamil Nadu, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Golden","family":"Nancy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science and Technology (AI & ML), Karunya Institute of Technology and Sciences, Coimbatore, Tamil Nadu, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014714600005024","published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,17]],"date-time":"2026-09-17T13:06:37Z","timestamp":1789650397000}},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>This paper investigates an efficient approach for distilling Large Language Models (LLMs) into smaller, application-specific models using zero-shot Chain of Thought (CoT) rationale generation and Optimization by Prompting (OPRO). To address the challenges of deploying computationally intensive generative AI for narrow tasks or resource-constrained environments, the approach leverages LLM reasoning capabilities to generate both labels and natural language explanations for unlabeled data. By reducing reliance on human-generated annotations, the approach substantially lowers annotation requirements and prompting costs while maintaining comparable performance in the evaluated settings. We formulate distillation as a multi-task learning problem in which student models are trained to jointly predict labels and learn from teacher-generated rationales, with the goal of improving data efficiency and generalization. Building on established zero-shot Chain of Thought (CoT) prompting and the OPRO prompt optimization technique, we use teacher-generated rationales to reduce annotation token requirements and examine the associated performance and efficiency gains. Additionally, we systematically investigate how explanation properties affect distillation efficiency. Across natural language inference and question answering benchmarks, results indicate that near-optimal performance can be achieved even when rationales are provided for only a subset of the training data, and that shorter explanations are often sufficient. These findings provide practical insights into the trade-offs between rationale generation cost and student model performance. Overall, this work contributes empirical evidence on the effectiveness and cost characteristics of rationale-based distillation for training compact, task-specific language models with minimal human intervention.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s44491-026-00021-w","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,2]],"date-time":"2026-09-02T12:09:25Z","timestamp":1788350965000},"title":["Toward scalable generative AI: efficient language model distillation via zero-shot rationales"],"author":[{"given":"Lukas","family":"V\u00f6ge","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1502-3670","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vincent","family":"Gurgul","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Stefan","family":"Lessmann","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s44491-026-00021-w.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/article\/10.1007\/s44491-026-00021-w","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s44491-026-00021-w.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s44491-026-00021-w","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,2]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Automated peripheral blood cell classification has shown strong performance on curated microscopic image datasets, but model reliability can decrease when images are acquired from external sources with different visual characteristics. This study presents a reproducible deep learning protocol for five-class peripheral blood cell morphology classification involving basophils, eosinophils, lymphocytes, monocytes and neutrophils. The protocol compared five pretrained architectures, including Swin-Tiny, ConvNeXt-Tiny, MaxViT-Tiny, DeiT-Small and EfficientNetV2-B0, using balanced PBC training, validation and locked-test splits, followed by external zero-shot testing, image-quality stress evaluation and external-domain calibration. Initial PBC-only training produced high internal test performance but substantially lower external zero-shot performance, indicating a dataset-shift problem. To address this, we developed a source-retaining dual-domain calibration strategy that combines external calibration data, PBC rehearsal samples and teacher distillation. DeiT-Small was selected as the calibration backbone because it provided the strongest balance between PBC performance, stress robustness and external zero-shot generalisation. On locked testing, the proposed approach achieved a macro-F1 score of 0.9933 on the PBC test set and 0.9746 on the external test set. External class-wise performance remained high across all five cell categories, with macro precision, recall and F1 of approximately 0.9747, 0.9747 and 0.9746, respectively. These findings suggest that source-retaining calibration can improve cross-dataset generalisation while preserving source-domain performance. The study provides a transparent evaluation framework for assessing robustness, external validity and domain-shift effects in microscopic blood-cell image classification.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.17762\/jidmis.v3.3213","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T06:39:27Z","timestamp":1791441567000},"title":["Dual-Domain Calibration with Source Rehearsal and Knowledge Distillation for Robust Cross-Dataset Classification"],"author":[{"family":"Trupti Kaushiram Wable","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jidmis.org\/index.php\/jidmis\/article\/download\/3213\/1409","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jidmis.org\/index.php\/jidmis\/article\/download\/3213\/1409","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.17762\/jidmis.v3.3213","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.compeleceng.2026.111503","title":["DBLP: Noise bridge consistency distillation for efficient and reliable adversarial purification"],"author":[{"given":"Chihan","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Belal","family":"Alsinglawi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Islam","family":"Al-qudah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.compeleceng.2026.111503","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,17]],"date-time":"2026-09-17T22:31:08Z","timestamp":1789684268000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0045790626005732?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0045790626005732?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7389081","title":["Content in Graph Clothing: Structural Link Prediction Does Not Beat a Text Baseline for Weakness Classification of Vulnerability Reports"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-7136-7168","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mohammadreza","family":"Rashidi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>A vulnerability report is a short text, a CVE description, and a common task is to assign it a weakness class, a CWE. One appealing way to do this is to build a graph, connect each report to the terms of its description and each weakness to the terms of its known reports, and predict a report-to-weakness link with a structural index such as Adamic-Adar that scores a candidate by its shared neighbours. We ask whether this graph machinery earns its keep, by measuring it against the plainest alternative, ranking weaknesses by the cosine similarity of the report text to known reports. On 560 real vulnerability reports across 7 weakness classes from the National Vulnerability Database, with five-fold cross-validation and held-out report-to-weakness links, the content baseline recovers the correct weakness with a recall at one of 0.838 and a mean reciprocal rank of 0.898, and every structural index is worse, common neighbours at 0.764, Jaccard at 0.761, and Adamic-Adar lowest at 0.616. Adding the structural score to the content baseline does not help, the ensemble reaches 0.832 against the baseline's 0.838, so the graph carries no signal the text does not, and a stronger supervised text classifier over the same features widens the gap to 0.921, so the structural indices lose to any reasonable text method. The reason is specific and mechanical. Because the graph is built from the report text, a structural link is a term-overlap score in disguise, and Adamic-Adar's rule of weighting a shared neighbour by the inverse log of its degree up-weights rare, off-topic terms and down-weights the diagnostic terms, sql, traversal, scripting, which occur in a moderate fraction of reports, a median of 15 percent, so its weighting is misaligned with the task. The conclusion is that when a graph is derived from content, structural link prediction is content similarity in graph clothing, and a worse-weighted one, so it should be measured against a text baseline before it is adopted. We release the corpus split and the harness.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7389081","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,5]],"date-time":"2026-09-05T02:48:46Z","timestamp":1788576526000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/taffc.2026.3737662","title":["An Efficient and Adaptive Multi-Teacher Distillation Framework for Toxic Speech Detection"],"author":[{"given":"Yongjie","family":"Gui","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Tongji University","place":["Shanghai, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hao","family":"Deng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tongji University","place":["Shanghai, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shengjie","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tongji University","place":["Shanghai, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/taffc.2026.3737662","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T19:26:36Z","timestamp":1790277996000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/5165369\/5520654\/11710248.pdf?arnumber=11710248","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.26599\/tst.2026.9010077","title":["AKDF: Adaptive knowledge distillation for secure, efficient, copyright-protected model publishing"],"author":[{"given":"Feng","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Honghui","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Daehee","family":"Seo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yongjoon","family":"Joe","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wonbin","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhipeng","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>The rise of large language models (LLMs) has transformed natural language processing, powering applications from creative writing to code generation. However, their vast size and proprietary nature present two major challenges, including efficient deployment on limited hardware and secure protection of intellectual property. This work introduces the Adaptive Knowledge Distillation Framework (AKDF), a unified training approach that simultaneously com-presses LLMs and embeds ownership signals for copyright assurance. AKDF employs parameter-efficient Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) to distill a 1-billion-parameter student model from an 8-billion-parameter teacher while integrating an output-level watermarking module directly into the distillation process. This design reduces trainable parameters and encodes verifiable ownership signatures without altering the frozen base weights. Experiments on ARC-Easy, PIQA, and WMT16 show that the student retains approximately 76% of the teacher\u2019s performance while maintaining competitive reasoning and translation quality under substantial compression. AKDF also enables secure and communication-efficient model publishing, providing a practical path to-ward bandwidth-efficient and ownership-aware deployment of large-scale language models.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26599\/tst.2026.9010077","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]],"date-time":"2026-09-08T09:27:25Z","timestamp":1788859645000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tlt.2026.3736330\/mm1","title":["Analysing Learning \u201cin the Wild\u201d: Modelling Individual Trajectories of Cognitive Engagement via Automated Text Classification and Social Network Analysis_supp1-3736330.pdf"],"author":[{"given":"Simon","family":"Krukowski","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tlt.2026.3736330\/mm1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T20:53:56Z","timestamp":1790196836000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.1109\/TLT.2026.3736330","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Stylometric features and frozen sentence-transformer embeddings are widely used as low-cost inputs for misinformation-related text classification, but heterogeneous benchmark tasks are rarely compared under a common protocol. We evaluated term frequency\u2013inverse document frequency (TF-IDF), a 22-feature stylometric battery, frozen sentence-BERT, and fine-tuned RoBERTa on 15 English-language benchmarks (n = 24,906), including five human\u2013AI provenance corpora. A descriptive partition of the raw area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) values attributed 71.8% of the total sum of squares to task category means and 13.7% to benchmarks within categories; an exact benchmark label permutation yielded p = 0.000161, but category remained confounded with benchmark construction, source, domain, label mapping, and sampling. After correcting three label orientations, the mean off-diagonal transfer AUC was 0.492 for stylometry and 0.507 for sentence-BERT. Retained-character matching reduced the length-only AUC to approximately 0.50, while low-cost methods retained AUCs of 0.779\u20130.999 in four estimable human\u2013AI benchmarks; AITextPile lacked common support. Alternative LIAR\/LIAR2 mappings changed individual estimates and method ordering. Random intake sensitivity was method-dependent: the MAGE length-only AUC fell from 0.936 to a median of 0.476 and AITextPile from 1.000 to 0.560. The results support benchmark- and target-domain-specific validation, not a universal misinformation or AI-text detector.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/info17090866","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]],"date-time":"2026-09-08T09:02:24Z","timestamp":1788858144000},"title":["Benchmark-Task Heterogeneity in Misinformation-Related and Human\u2013AI Text Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Grzegorz","family":"\u015awierk","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Humanities, AGH University of Krakow, ul. Czarnowiejska 36, 30-054 Krak\u00f3w, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"visualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"formal-analysis","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"data-curation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4494-3890","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rafa\u0142","family":"Olszowski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Humanities, AGH University of Krakow, ul. Czarnowiejska 36, 30-054 Krak\u00f3w, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2078-2489\/17\/9\/866\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/info17090866","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]]}},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s00034-026-03770-5","title":["DKDN: Dual Kernel Distillation Network for Efficient Image Super-Resolution"],"author":[{"given":"Yinggan","family":"Tang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuchen","family":"Shi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Leichao","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yiwei","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s00034-026-03770-5","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T19:11:27Z","timestamp":1789585887000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s00034-026-03770-5.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/article\/10.1007\/s00034-026-03770-5","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s00034-026-03770-5.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Deep neural networks (DNNs) have achieved remarkable success in medical image classification, yet their performance remains sensitive to dataset size. Knowledge distillation (KD) alleviates this issue by transferring knowledge from a high-capacity teacher to a lightweight student. However, conventional KD relies on a static teacher, while adaptive teacher updating may improve performance on the student-learning data while reducing retention of knowledge acquired during teacher pretraining. To address these limitations, we propose an Anti-forgetting Adaptive Teacher-driven Knowledge Distillation framework (A2T-KD), which aims to balance teacher adaptation and pretraining-knowledge retention. The proposed framework integrates three modules: MITR for cross-epoch representation consistency, DSDO for prediction-space decoupling and class discriminability, and SGKD for feature- and logit-level knowledge transfer. Across nine medical imaging datasets, A2T-KD achieved higher mean values than the fixed-teacher Vanilla KD baseline in 30 of 36 dataset\u2013metric comparisons. It also exhibited the lowest pretraining-set ACC degradation among the evaluated teacher-update baselines on all nine datasets, supporting the intended balance between teacher adaptation and pretraining-knowledge retention under the evaluated settings.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/app16178756","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T11:08:53Z","timestamp":1788433733000},"title":["Anti-Forgetting Adaptive Teacher-Driven Knowledge Distillation for Medical Image Classification"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5903-3902","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tao","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Radiology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"resources","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0609-1658","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chuan","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Radiology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"data-curation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"resources","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-4677-9514","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yifan","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Radiology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2069-8066","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lubomir M.","family":"Hadjiiski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Radiology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1038-2116","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Qian","family":"Dong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Radiology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"visualization","vocabulary":"credit"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2076-3417\/16\/17\/8756\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/app16178756","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]]}},{"DOI":"10.54499\/2025.00290.cpca.a3","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54499\/2025.00290.cpca.a3","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T22:09:30Z","timestamp":1790633370000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mapr72750.2026.11685733","title":["Efficient Model Pruning via Selective Layer-wise Distillation with Dynamic CKA-based Weighting"],"author":[{"given":"Chi-Dieu","family":"Bui","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Sciences, Hue University","place":["Hue, Vietnam"],"department":["Department of Information Technology"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chien-Quang","family":"Le","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Sciences, Hue University","place":["Hue, Vietnam"],"department":["Department of Information Technology"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mapr72750.2026.11685733","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T19:24:08Z","timestamp":1789586648000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11685436\/11685665\/11685733.pdf?arnumber=11685733","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icivc70586.2026.11686546","title":["Dual-Teacher Personalized Federated Learning with Hyper-Knowledge Distillation for Breast Ultrasound Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Yuelei","family":"Hou","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Henan Medical University","place":["Xinxiang, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lijie","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Henan Medical University","place":["Xinxiang, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zongya","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Henan Medical University","place":["Xinxiang, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shouying","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Henan Medical University","place":["Xinxiang, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icivc70586.2026.11686546","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T19:21:06Z","timestamp":1789586466000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11686486\/11686487\/11686546.pdf?arnumber=11686546","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.24200\/sci.2026.67287.10541","title":["Real-Time Fine-Grained Tangerine Classification via Knowledge Distillation of an Optimized YOLOv7 Model on STM32H743 Microcontroller"],"author":[{"given":"Farhad","family":"Hassanzadeh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sara","family":"Ershadi-Nasab","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.24200\/sci.2026.67287.10541","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,30]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,27]],"date-time":"2026-09-27T08:19:56Z","timestamp":1790497196000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/scientiairanica.sharif.edu\/article_24224_9dfb3669898384fee5db884301163eb3.pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7513373","title":["Universal Noise Augmentation (UNA): A Unified Training-Time Framework for Robust Multilingual Text Classification"],"author":[{"given":"AliReza","family":"Ghasemi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-5957-7145","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Behrouz  Minaei","family":"Bidgoli","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-0686-1498","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Aida","family":"Khaleghi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Reliable multilingual text classification systems must tolerate surface-level noise without losing clean-input performance. Existing robustness toolkits provide perturbation libraries and evaluation workflows, but they do not by themselves define a compact training-time protocol that jointly controls perturbation scope, severity, sampling, and clean-noisy exposure. We introduce Universal Noise Augmentation (UNA), a unified training-time framework that integrates character-, token-, and structural-level perturbations with configurable intensity, clean-noisy mixing, operator sampling, and epoch-aware dynamic resampling. UNA is a protocol-level contribution rather than a claim of new primitive corruption operators, and it requires no modification to the classifier architecture or loss. We evaluate UNA with XLM-RoBERTa-base in three multilingual classification settings: cross-lingual natural language inference (MultiNLI\u2192XNLI), intent classification (MASSIVE), and sentiment classification (Amazon Reviews Multilingual). Evaluation separates clean data, an in-family synthetic-noise stress test, and fixed task-specific noisy subsets, and includes ablations of intensity, clean-noisy ratio, sampling strategy, and dynamic versus static augmentation. Across six noisy task-tier comparisons, UNA improves accuracy by 6.8\u20138.5 percentage points, while clean accuracy changes by at most 0.4 percentage points. The results support UNA as a practical, architecture-preserving robustness-training configuration for engineering multilingual classification pipelines under the evaluated conditions.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7513373","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T17:09:33Z","timestamp":1790183373000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) can acquire clear and reliable radar images under complex weather conditions such as rain and fog, offering significant research and application value in maritime surveillance, port monitoring, and ship target recognition. However, existing SAR ship target classification models still face challenges of large parameter counts and low inference efficiency in edge computing scenarios, and the classification performance for certain target categories remains unsatisfactory in practical situations involving limited samples and imbalanced data. To address these issues, this paper adopts a lightweight SAR ship target classification approach that combines VV-VH dual-polarization feature fusion with model compression. Using OpenSARShip_uint8 as the experimental dataset, the method first constructs dual-channel inputs that leverage the complementary scattering response information from VV and VH polarization channels to enhance the model's ability to represent ship structural and textural features, thereby improving classification stability. Second, to build a high-quality teacher model, this paper employs VGG16 pre-trained on ImageNet and designs a two-stage training strategy: in the first stage, the feature extraction layers are frozen and only the classifier is trained; in the second stage, all network parameters are unfrozen, and a differentiated learning rate strategy is applied for fine-tuning the entire network-a smaller learning rate is used for the feature extraction layers to preserve pre-trained features, while a relatively larger learning rate is used for the classifier to accelerate adaptation, combined with cosine annealing scheduling to gradually decay the learning rate. This strategy helps improve the model's capacity to represent fine-grained features in SAR images and mitigates, to a certain extent, the model bias caused by data imbalance. Furthermore, knowledge distillation is employed to transfer the feature learning capability of the teacher model to a lightweight student model, significantly reducing the model size while preserving recognition accuracy as much as possible. Experimental results show that the teacher model achieves a test accuracy of 74.08%, while the distilled student model attains a test accuracy of 73.86% with only a 0.22% accuracy degradation. Despite the moderate absolute classification accuracy limited by speckle noise, inter-class scattering ambiguity and dataset imbalance, the proposed distillation framework drastically shrinks the model scale: parameters drop from 18.92M to 1.24M, storage footprint falls from 72.17MB to 4.74MB, and single-image inference latency decreases from 1.93ms to 0.71ms.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.54691\/1z7hk196","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T05:04:42Z","timestamp":1791867882000},"title":["A Lightweight SAR Ship Classification Method based on VV\/VH Dual-Polarization Fusion and Knowledge Distillation"],"author":[{"given":"Xuanyu","family":"Bai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/bcpublication.org\/index.php\/SJISR\/article\/download\/9666\/9597","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/bcpublication.org\/index.php\/SJISR\/article\/download\/9666\/9597","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54691\/1z7hk196","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.patcog.2026.114767","title":["FEDS: Feature and Entropy-Based Distillation Strategy for efficient learned image compression"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8709-5927","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Haisheng","family":"Fu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jie","family":"Liang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Feng","family":"Liang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guohe","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jingning","family":"Han","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.patcog.2026.114767","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,3]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T23:09:37Z","timestamp":1788995377000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0031320326017310?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0031320326017310?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Acoustic and image sensors in unattended ground sensor systems often operate at different sampling rates and use different trigger mechanisms and fields of view, so their observations are asynchronous and lack instance-level correspondence. In addition, web images used to supplement scarce field annotations exhibit a substantial domain shift from target-domain images. This paper proposes a three-stream joint learning framework that integrates reliability-weighted image-domain adaptation, reliability-aware dynamic bidirectional prototype distillation, and balanced complementary modality dropout. A lightweight shared backbone processes labeled acoustic spectra, labeled mixed-source images, and unlabeled target-domain images. A main classifier and two auxiliary classifiers estimate target pseudo-label confidence and agreement. Their consensus is used to construct reliability weights for marginal alignment, while confidence guides target-sample selection and weighting for class-conditional alignment. Acoustic and image class prototypes transfer category-level knowledge without constructing artificial instance pairs, while direction-specific distillation weights are dynamically assigned according to classification reliability, prototype availability, and prototype stability. Across three independent runs with different random seeds, experiments on datasets comprising 148,038 acoustic spectra and 167,754 images yield acoustic, image, and overall accuracies of 91.97\u00b10.08%, 95.16\u00b10.14%, and 93.72\u00b10.07%, respectively, exceeding the highest corresponding baseline mean accuracies by 1.52, 1.30, and 2.31 percentage points. The deployment model contains 123.40 K parameters and requires 0.47 MiB in FP32, making it a compact classification model with potential for deployment in resource-constrained field environments.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/app16199568","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]],"date-time":"2026-09-25T16:31:14Z","timestamp":1790353874000},"title":["Asynchronous Acoustic\u2013Image Classification for Unattended Ground Sensors with Domain Adaptation and Bidirectional Prototype Distillation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0552-6194","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yan","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory of Heterogeneous Integrated Microsystem, SIMIT, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shanghai 200050, China"},{"name":"University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"formal-analysis","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"data-curation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"visualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"funding-acquisition","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"given":"Lijie","family":"Xia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory of Heterogeneous Integrated Microsystem, SIMIT, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shanghai 200050, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"data-curation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"given":"Jun","family":"Pei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory of Heterogeneous Integrated Microsystem, SIMIT, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shanghai 200050, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"investigation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7243-9229","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Baoqing","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory of Heterogeneous Integrated Microsystem, SIMIT, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shanghai 200050, China"},{"name":"University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"conceptualization","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"resources","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"project-administration","vocabulary":"credit"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2076-3417\/16\/19\/9568\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/app16199568","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]]}},{"DOI":"10.32388\/6hk3ta","title":["Review of: \"SAS Multimetric Tribunal: Structural Classification of Hallucination in AI-Generated Text \u2013 What a Benchmark Design Can and Cannot Show\""],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9120-7298","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Evgenii","family":"Arsentev","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/6hk3ta","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T08:49:41Z","timestamp":1791362981000},"relation":{"is-review-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.32388\/T420IV.4","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/6HK3TA\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"unspecified"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.qeios.com\/read\/6HK3TA\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/conecct68978.2026.11700740","title":["Open-Set Text Classification with Limited Labelling Budget"],"author":[{"given":"Amit","family":"Chaulwar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Samsung R and D","place":["Bangalore"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/conecct68978.2026.11700740","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:15:27Z","timestamp":1790622927000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11700512\/11700570\/11700740.pdf?arnumber=11700740","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202609.2665.v1","title":["Reliability in Emotion Classification, Sentiment Analysis, and AI-Generated Text Detection: A Systematic Review"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-9317-340X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nisreen","family":"Albzour","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sarah S.","family":"Lam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Text classification systems are increasingly deployed in settings where a confident but incorrect prediction carries a real cost. Aggregate accuracy on a held-out split says little about whether such a system can be trusted. This systematic review examines how reliability is pursued and evidenced across emotion classification, sentiment analysis, and AI-generated text detection. Following PRISMA, seven electronic databases (ScienceDirect, Scopus, Web of Science, ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, Compendex, and PubMed) were searched together with a supplementary Google Scholar search. The search covered text-only journal articles and conference papers published from January 2016 through July 2026 that addressed a relevant NLP task and evaluated or improved at least one reliability dimension. Of 1,797 records identified, 1,294 were screened after deduplication, 464 were assessed in full text, and 51 studies met all criteria. Each study was characterized using a five-dimensional taxonomy: Task Category, Model Type, Reliability Approaches, Reliability Evaluation Evidence, and Reliability Validation. Task Category is mutually exclusive; the remaining four dimensions are non-mutually exclusive. Dataset language was recorded separately. Across the 51 included studies, AI-generated text detection accounts for 28 studies, sentiment analysis for 15, and emotion classification for 8. Encoder-Based Pre-trained Language Models (PLMs) are the most common Model Type (35 studies), followed by Decoder-Based LLMs (25) and Other Deep-Learning Architectures (18). Reliability Approaches are led by Uncertainty Estimation (15 studies), followed by Selective Prediction (9), Calibration (7), Distance and Representation Methods (7), Statistical and Likelihood Methods (5), and Consistency and Agreement Methods (3). Their distribution differs sharply by task category: Under the operational coding definition used in this systematic review, Uncertainty Estimation appears in 12 sentiment studies and 3 emotion studies, while no AI-generated-text study met the criteria for this category. All 5 Statistical and Likelihood Methods studies occur in AI-generated text detection, a concentration that partly reflects the category definition, since likelihood-based provenance statistics are themselves a detection technique. Reliability Evaluation Evidence is most often Error-Rate and Operating-Point Evidence (19 studies), followed by Calibration Evidence (14), Selective Prediction and Coverage Evidence (10), and Uncertainty Evidence (9). Reliability Validation is also uneven: 24 studies include Out-of-Distribution Validation, 21 Adversarial and Perturbation Validation, 20 Cross-Generator Generalization, 4 Cross-Lingual Validation, while 18 are limited to In-Distribution testing. The review 2 contributes a unified five-dimensional framework that makes the application, model context, reliability mechanism, supporting evidence, and validation conditions directly comparable across the three task categories and exposes where evidence remains narrower than the reliability claim it is used to support.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202609.2665.v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T14:24:37Z","timestamp":1790864677000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>A key obstacle to bringing computer vision everywhere, from robots and drones to smart cities and wearable tech, is the difficulty of deploying real-time object detectors on resource-limited edge hardware such as the NVIDIA Jetson Nano, Raspberry Pi 5, and ARM-based mobile processors. State-of-the-art detectors such as YOLOv8-L achieve outstanding accuracy on server GPUs but often deliver only single-digit or low FPS on embedded hardware. This paper presents EdgeKD-Net, a multi-signal Knowledge Distillation (KD) framework that transfers the representational knowledge of a high-capacity teacher to a compact edge-deployable student. The student employs a MobileNetV3-Large backbone augmented with a quantization- and distillation-aware Dual-Path Feature Pyramid (DPFP). Three complementary distillation signals are introduced: (i) temperature-scaled response-level KL divergence (?=4); (ii) Channel-Affinity Feature Alignment (CAFA), transferring structural channel co-activation patterns via cosine-affinity matrices; and (iii) Detection Fidelity Loss (DFL), applied exclusively on the formally defined consistent anchor set, eliminating noisy gradients from anchor-assignment mismatch. Across three independent runs, EdgeKD-Net achieves a mAP of 38.6 \u00b1 0.14 mAP@[0.50:0.95] on MS-COCO 2017, only 1.4 mAP below its YOLOv8-L teacher while operating at 61 FPS (FP16) and 89 FPS (INT8) on NVIDIA Jetson Nano with 8.77 mAP\/W energy efficiency. The framework is also evaluated on the VisDrone 2023 dataset, which contains dense aerial small objects. It achieves 14.1 \u00b1 0.22 mAP@[0.50:0.95], compared with 12.4 for the best distillation baseline. This improvement is larger than the 0.9-point gain on MS-COCO, showing that the proposed method is effective on both datasets. The evaluation is limited to these two datasets, and we do not claim broader generalization.\nABSTRAK: Halangan utama dalam memperluas penggunaan penglihatan komputer, daripada robot dan dron kepada bandar pintar serta teknologi boleh pakai, ialah kesukaran melaksanakan pengesan objek masa nyata pada perkakasan pengkomputeran pinggir yang mempunyai sumber terhad, seperti NVIDIA Jetson Nano, Raspberry Pi 5 dan pemproses mudah alih berasaskan ARM. Pengesan tercanggih seperti YOLOv8-L mencapai ketepatan yang sangat baik pada unit pemprosesan grafik (GPU) pelayan, tetapi lazimnya hanya menghasilkan kadar bingkai sesaat (FPS) satu digit atau rendah apabila dilaksanakan pada perkakasan terbenam. Makalah ini memperkenalkan EdgeKD-Net, iaitu kerangka Penyulingan Pengetahuan (*Knowledge Distillation*, KD) berbilang isyarat yang memindahkan pengetahuan representasi daripada model pengajar berkapasiti tinggi kepada model pelajar kompak yang sesuai dilaksanakan pada peranti pinggir. Model pelajar menggunakan tulang belakang MobileNetV3-Large yang dipertingkatkan dengan Piramid Ciri Dwialiran (*Dual-Path Feature Pyramid*, DPFP) yang peka terhadap pengkuantuman dan penyulingan. Tiga isyarat penyulingan yang saling melengkapi diperkenalkan: (i) pencapahan Kullback\u2013Leibler (KL) pada aras respons dengan penskalaan suhu (? = 4); (ii) Penjajaran Ciri Afiniti Saluran (*Channel-Affinity Feature Alignment*, CAFA), yang memindahkan pola struktur pengaktifan bersama saluran melalui matriks afiniti kosinus; dan (iii) Kehilangan Kesetiaan Pengesanan (*Detection Fidelity Loss*, DFL), yang digunakan secara eksklusif pada set sauh tekal yang ditakrifkan secara formal bagi menghapuskan kecerunan hingar akibat ketidakpadanan penetapan sauh. Berdasarkan tiga larian bebas, EdgeKD-Net mencapai mAP sebanyak 38.6 \u00b1 0.14 bagi mAP@[0.50:0.95] pada set data MS-COCO 2017, iaitu hanya 1.4 mata mAP lebih rendah daripada model pengajarnya, YOLOv8-L. Pada NVIDIA Jetson Nano, model ini beroperasi pada 61 FPS menggunakan FP16 dan 89 FPS menggunakan INT8, dengan kecekapan tenaga sebanyak 8.77 mAP\/W. Kerangka ini turut dinilai menggunakan set data VisDrone 2023 yang mengandungi objek udara bersaiz kecil dan padat. EdgeKD-Net mencapai 14.1 \u00b1 0.22 mAP@[0.50:0.95], berbanding 12.4 yang dicatatkan oleh kaedah asas penyulingan terbaik. Peningkatan ini lebih besar daripada peningkatan 0.9 mata pada MS-COCO, sekali gus menunjukkan bahawa kaedah yang dicadangkan berkesan pada kedua-dua set data. Walau bagaimanapun, penilaian kajian ini terhad kepada kedua-dua set data tersebut dan tiada dakwaan dibuat mengenai keupayaan generalisasi yang lebih luas.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.31436\/iiumej.v27i3.4344","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T14:33:52Z","timestamp":1789137232000},"title":["Efficient Edge-Based Object Detection via Multi-Signal Teacher\u2013Student Knowledge Distillation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8058-8665","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Richa","family":"Sharma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ashutosh","family":"Gupta","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Prasun","family":"Chakrabarti","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6291-1318","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Arvind","family":"Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/journals.iium.edu.my\/ejournal\/index.php\/iiumej\/article\/download\/4344\/1338","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/journals.iium.edu.my\/ejournal\/index.php\/iiumej\/article\/download\/4344\/1338","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31436\/iiumej.v27i3.4344","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]]}},{"DOI":"10.1134\/s1054661826700276","title":["Iterative Layerwise Distillation for Efficient Compression of Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Grigory","family":"Kovalev","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mikhail","family":"Tikhomirov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1134\/s1054661826700276","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,6]],"date-time":"2026-09-06T14:58:31Z","timestamp":1788706711000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1134\/S1054661826700276.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/article\/10.1134\/S1054661826700276","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1134\/S1054661826700276.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.ins.2026.124241","title":["Attention-based experience replay for knowledge distillation: an efficient model-agnostic continual learning strategy for multivariate time series forecasting"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-5377-1649","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Quentin","family":"Besnard","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2321-942X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nicolas","family":"Ragot","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.ins.2026.124241","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,2]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,3]],"date-time":"2026-10-03T15:01:18Z","timestamp":1791039678000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0020025526011722?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0020025526011722?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.25163\/data.7110689","title":["HCNN-LSTM with BERT Embeddings and TomekLinks for Imbalanced News Text Classification"],"abstract":"<jats:p>Text classification continues to occupy a central place in natural language processing research. Still, its effectiveness can diminish rather noticeably when datasets contain uneven category distributions. The consequence, perhaps unsurprisingly, is that minority categories\u2014despite sometimes carrying important or nuanced information\u2014tend to be overlooked or misclassified. This imbalance poses a persistent challenge for many real-world text analytics applications. In the present study, we explored this issue by developing a hybrid deep learning framework, referred to as HCNN-LSTM, designed specifically for imbalanced news text classification. The model integrates convolutional neural networks and long short-term memory networks with contextual BERT embeddings and the TomekLinks undersampling technique. The BBC News dataset, which contains 2,225 articles distributed across five categories\u2014business, entertainment, politics, sport, and technology\u2014served as the experimental benchmark for evaluating the proposed approach. Before training the models, a structured preprocessing pipeline was applied. This included text normalization, stop-word removal, lemmatization, and feature preparation, followed by class balancing procedures. The overall goal of this process was to reduce noise while preserving meaningful linguistic patterns within the dataset. Architecturally, the hybrid model was designed to capture both short-range lexical cues and longer contextual dependencies, while BERT-based embeddings provided richer semantic representations of textual content. On the imbalanced dataset, the model achieved an accuracy of 0.95 and a macro-averaged F1-score of 0.95. When evaluated on a balanced version of the dataset, performance improved further, reaching 0.99 for both accuracy and macro-averaged F1-score. Additional analysis using mean squared error indicated that the proposed hybrid architecture produced the lowest prediction error among all compared approaches. Taken together, these results suggest that combining contextual embeddings with hybrid neural architectures may provide a practical and effective strategy for improving classification performance in imbalanced text datasets.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25163\/data.7110689","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,14]],"date-time":"2026-09-14T15:33:07Z","timestamp":1789399987000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/publishing.emanresearch.org\/Journal\/Abstract\/data-7110689","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.23919\/micciscps00015.2026.00036","title":["Communication-Efficient Data-Free Federated Knowledge Distillation"],"author":[{"given":"Chengkai","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Pengchao","family":"Han","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tianliang","family":"Xia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chuan","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xu","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.23919\/micciscps00015.2026.00036","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:35:51Z","timestamp":1790624151000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11713084\/11713085\/11713223.pdf?arnumber=11713223","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>We present a lossless compression algorithm for indexing a protein database while supporting fast taxonomic classification in the method Centrifuger. The algorithm is a new scheme of the previously proposed run-block compression algorithm to reduce the size of the Ferragina-Manzini (FM) index, and it scales better with alphabet size than the original. On the RefSeq prokaryotic and viral protein sequences, Centrifuger reduces the memory footprint by over a third compared to the method Kaiju that builds on a plain FM-index, while having comparable running time. Furthermore, the compressed FM-index is lossless and can locate matches of arbitrary length, which helps Centrifuger achieve greater accuracy than Kraken2, a k-mer-based taxonomic classification method. We leverage the computational efficiency of Centrifuger to create an index of size 182 GB for classifying the reads against the full nr database that contains about 250 billion amino acid characters. Using this index, Centrifuger reveals different SARS-CoV-2 infection states and viral transcriptome profiles across human cell types from single-cell RNA-seq data.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.64898\/2026.09.17.752512","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T20:05:11Z","timestamp":1790280311000},"title":["Lossless compression of protein databases for efficient and accurate metagenomic sequence classification with Centrifuger"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0180-7426","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Li","family":"Song","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biomedical Data Science, Dartmouth College, Hanover, NH; Email: Li.Song@dartmouth.edu"},{"name":"Department of Computer Science, Dartmouth College, Hanover, NH"},{"name":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Dartmouth College, Hanover, NH"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/syndication.highwire.org\/content\/doi\/10.64898\/2026.09.17.752512","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64898\/2026.09.17.752512","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tgrs.2026.3738766\/mm1","title":["2DTS-MixNet: A Lightweight Mixing Network for Efficient Crop Classification across Diverse Cropping Systems_supp1-3738766.docx"],"author":[{"given":"Zhe","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tgrs.2026.3738766\/mm1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T19:18:36Z","timestamp":1790882316000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.1109\/TGRS.2026.3738766","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Lightweight sentence embedding models are critical for large-scale retrieval and semantic matching, yet aggressive compression often disrupts the semantic geometry learned by powerful teachers. Existing distillation methods primarily rely on point-wise feature matching or task-specific supervision, which fails to preserve fine-grained relational structure across samples. In this work, we propose Cross-layer Gram Geometry Distillation, a geometry-centric framework that transfers semantic structure by aligning pairwise similarity relations across teacher and student layers. By projecting multi-layer representations into a unified hyperspherical space and matching their Gram matrices under a cross-layer weighting mechanism, the student is optimized to reconstruct the teacher\u2019s neighborhood geometry. An additional covariance regularizer is introduced to mitigate representation collapse and anisotropy. Extensive experiments on sentence embedding benchmarks demonstrate that the proposed method consistently outperforms existing distillation baselines under strict parameter and latency constraints. Theoretical analysis further establishes that minimizing Gram discrepancy yields explicit guarantees on nearest-neighbor preservation, providing a principled foundation for retrieval-oriented lightweight embedding reconstruction.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1142\/s0218001426400665","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]],"date-time":"2026-09-30T04:06:41Z","timestamp":1790741201000},"title":["CL-GGD: Cross-layer Semantic Geometry Distillation for Lightweight Text Embedding Reconstruction"],"author":[{"given":"Chen","family":"Peng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiangbo","family":"Kong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-7303-8752","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xue","family":"Qiao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"He","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qiming","family":"Fu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.worldscientific.com\/doi\/pdf\/10.1142\/S0218001426400665","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1142\/s0218001426400665","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icimtech202671078.2026.11699061","title":["Efficient Cross-Domain Sentiment Analysis via Knowledge Distillation and Maximum Mean Discrepancy Alignment"],"author":[{"given":"Ummu Fatihah Mohd","family":"Bahrin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Universiti Teknologi MARA, Terengganu Branch, Campus Kuala Terengganu","place":["Kuala Terengganu, Terengganu, Malaysia"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sharifalillah","family":"Nordin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing Sciences, Universiti Teknologi MARA","place":["Shah Alam, Selangor, Malaysia"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Norlina Mohd","family":"Sabri","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universiti Teknologi MARA, Terengganu Branch, Campus Kuala Terengganu","place":["Kuala Terengganu, Terengganu, Malaysia"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icimtech202671078.2026.11699061","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:32:31Z","timestamp":1790623951000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11698891\/11698909\/11699061.pdf?arnumber=11699061","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}